pmc:AbandonmentAfterFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zwei Praktiker, die das Instrument nach dem Versagen aufgaben"@de,
        "Two practitioners who gave up the instrument after it failed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. VIII, Of the Religion of the Laplanders, p. 33" ;
    skos:definition "Two cases are reported together. A man of a Lapp village whose reindeer were dying prayed to his idol, strewed fir boughs about it, offered the skins, horns and skulls of reindeer, and prostrated himself with his whole family beseeching it to testify by some sign that it was the true god; after a whole day of prayers and devotions, finding no sign given, he set fire to the wood he had brought and burned the idol down, and asked the neighbours who sought to kill him why they would not let the god avenge himself. And a man whose mother was grievously sick sought remedy from the drum; his mother died notwithstanding, whereupon he cut his drum in pieces, alleging that he saw no use of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Both cases have the shape a protocol has: a question, a stated period, an outcome that could have fallen either way, and an act that follows from the outcome. In the first the period is a whole day and the demanded sign is named in advance. Neither man explains the failure away, and that is the whole point of entering them: it is the counter-case to a transmission that meets every disappointment with a named excuse."@en,
        "The provenance is the reason no more is made of them. Both stand in the chapter on the reception of Christianity, both are credited to a named priest of the mission, and both end with the man becoming a Christian or being praised as one. A missionary reporting that heathens destroyed their own idols after they failed is reporting the thing he most wanted to find. The cases are entered as claims standing in the work, and what they are evidence of is a separate question from what they say."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:AbsorptionThresholdDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie hoch die Schwelle der Vertiefung liegt"@de,
        "How high the threshold of absorption stands"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga XII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:definition "A disagreement about what is required for absorption. The discourses describe it as reached in the ordinary course of the training; the handbook sets a far higher bar, remarking that only one in a hundred or a thousand accomplishes it."@en ;
    skos:note "The disagreement bears directly on every prerequisite in this file that runs through the fourth absorption. If the handbook's threshold is the right one, the capacities it makes conditional on that absorption are conditional on something very few reach; if the discourses' is, they are not."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:FourAbsorptions .

pmc:AbsorptionsAreGraded a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Jede Vertiefung setzt die vorhergehende voraus"@de,
        "Each absorption presupposes the one before it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Dīgha Nikāya 2, Sāmaññaphala Sutta (trans. Ṭhānissaro Bhikkhu)" ;
    skos:note "The series is strictly graded in both canon and handbook, each stage defined by what it has let go of. No passage was found that forbids skipping in so many words, which is why this is recorded as an order and not as a necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:FourthJhana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:FourAbsorptions ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:AcaraLadder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die aufsteigende Ordnung der ācāras"@de,
        "The ascending order of the ācāras"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kulārṇava Tantra, in the section on the superiority of the Kula-dharma, at the verses giving the order of superiority of the ācāras (text with English translation by Ram Kumar Rai, Varanasi 1999)" ;
    skos:definition "A graded order of ways of practice in which each is declared superior to the one before: the Veda above all others, the Vaiṣṇava above the Veda, the Śaiva above the Vaiṣṇava, the Dakṣiṇa above the Śaiva, the Vāma above the Dakṣiṇa, the Siddhānta above the Vāma, the Kaula above the Siddhānta, and nothing above the Kaula."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter and verse are not recoverable from the copy read, which is an OCR of a printed translation whose running structure the scan does not carry reliably. Work, section and edition are named instead, which meets the standard of findability; a later reader working from another edition should expect a different numbering. See prima-materia#45 and prima-materia#56."@en,
        "This is the passage Wilson translates in 1832 without naming a place in the work, and on which pmc:RightAndLeftHandNomenclature rests. It is now located in an edition, so the order is a claim of the tantra and no longer only a claim of its reporter."@en,
        "Two things follow that the Western pair of names cannot carry. The order has seven steps and not two, so a scheme with two is not a simplification of it but a replacement. And within the order the left stands above the right, so the English rendering keeps the pair and reverses its valuation. Whoever explains the modern left-hand path out of this passage explains it out of a text that ranks the left higher than the right and both below two further steps."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:AccuserPaysOnAnAcquittal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Freigesprochene darf den Ankläger büßen"@de,
        "The acquitted may fine his accuser"@en ;
    dcterms:source "John H. Weeks, Among the Primitive Bakongo (London: Seeley, Service and Co., 1914), p. 263 and p. 264" ;
    skos:definition "A man who has passed the ordeal is led back to the town with songs of praise and dressed in fine cloth, and the corpus states that he may then mulct his accuser in a very heavy fine; in the milder form used for theft and adultery, the accused who retains the dose may demand compensation from his accusers."@en ;
    skos:note "A cost on the accuser is the one feature of the procedure that works against its abuse, and it is worth recording that the transmission has it. The second witness records the cost running the other way at the same point, since in some districts the ordeal-giver loses his fee when the accused vomits, so the two incentives are opposed within one procedure."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:AcquittalDeniedByAscribingEvasion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ein Freispruch, den man durch behauptete Umgehung entwertet"@de,
        "An acquittal denied by ascribing evasion"@en ;
    dcterms:source "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), pp. 147-148" ;
    skos:definition "A named case from Loango. A man was divined to be the owner of the leopard that had killed another man's wife; he denied it, was told by the ruler that nobody would believe him and that the decision lay with God, went to another town, demanded the ordeal, took it and vomited, and was thereby innocent. His accusers held instead that he had used his knowledge of the black art to escape the proper effect of the bark, and left their own town rather than live beside him."@en ;
    skos:note "The counterpart within the ordeal to the failure attributions on the charged things: an outcome that clears a man is not accepted as clearing him, and the exit is a capacity ascribed to him which the procedure cannot test. Recorded with the case because the case is named and dated by the recorder and can be argued with."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:KongoNkisi .

pmc:ActionSansSensation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wirken, das keine Empfindung hervorbringt"@de,
        "The action that produces no sensation at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, p. 42" ;
    skos:definition "It frequently happens that the operation gradually reestablishes the harmony of the system without producing any sensation, its influence being perceived only in the restoration of health. In that case the operator is to continue zealously, without troubling himself about the manner in which it acts and without seeking any apparent effect; by making efforts of attention and of will, or trying processes thought more active, he would fatigue himself uselessly and perhaps disturb the gradual and peaceful course of nature."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the clause that forbids the only response an operator might make to a null result — trying harder or trying differently — on the ground that it would disturb the process. A transmission that treats absence of effect as evidence of a quiet effect and forbids intensification has no state left in which it is not working."@en,
        "The second cancelling clause, and the one that empties the five-day rule from inside. That rule says the presumption of no action may not be founded on the patient having felt nothing; this passage says that feeling nothing is a normal course of successful action. Between them, no observation the patient can make within the term counts either way."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:AddressWithoutSight a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Haupt-Huaca wird angesprochen, ohne gesehen zu werden"@de,
        "The principal huaca is addressed without being seen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II and cap. V" ;
    skos:definition "Not all who address the huaca see it. Although everyone knows in which direction it stands, few see it, because they hang back and only the priest goes forward, speaks and offers. Places where a huaca is said to have rested, called zamana, and places from which it is invoked, called cayan, are reverenced too."@en ;
    skos:note "In one village a woman who kept the huaca told the people to cover their eyes when they adored it, because the divine essence cannot be seen with bodily eyes. That is the transmission stating a reason for the arrangement, and it is entered because a reason given from inside is worth more than the recorder's explanation, which is that the devil had put fear into them."@en,
        "The arrangement has a consequence the recorder reports with satisfaction and which is a finding whoever reports it: when the villagers were finally made to look at what they had feared, that was itself the strongest argument against it. A cult whose object is addressed without being seen is a cult that can be undone by showing the object."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Über die Zulässigkeit des Vorgangs entscheidet das Datum und keine der beiden Seiten"@de,
        "The date, and not either party, decides whether the operation may be done"@en ;
    skos:note "A counter-doctrine was found on 2026-09-11 and is recorded here rather than in a file of its own, because it bounds this statement without refuting it. The internal alchemy denies the binding in the stream where the binding is otherwise general: the thirteenth quatrain of the Jindan sizi holds that the fire phases use no hour and that the winter solstice is not at zi, and its commentary adds that one may not be bound to the four seasons. That is not a transmission that binds an operation to a calendar and would have to say where the failure lies; it is a transmission that refuses to bind and puts the condition in the practitioner. The statement above is about the first kind and this is the second, so the antecedent is not met — but a stream that contains both is a reminder that the class is not a property of a culture. What makes the case sharp is that the same corpus holds both positions: the Zhong-Lü chuandao ji binds throughout and makes ignorance of the times a sufficient cause of total failure, and the Wuzhen pian does both within one juan of one recension. See pmc:NeidanHuohouDispute."@en,
        "A fourth witness was added on 2026-09-11 from the Zhen'gao, and it does not merely repeat the others. Its prohibited days are given with the reckoning behind them, the woman's palace in shen and the man's in yin standing in mutual punishment, so the condition is located in the date and in the relation of the two signs and in neither party; and the passage adds that the person does not notice the harm while the body has already been damaged, which places the failure beyond the reach of either party's report as well. That is the statement above in its strongest available form."@en,
        "A seventh witness was added on 2026-09-18, and it tests the statement rather than repeating it. The demotic manual of London and Leiden binds nine of its operations to a time — a half-month of the moon from the fourth day to the fifteenth, the fifteenth day when the moon fills the uzat, the third and the fourteenth of the lunar month, midday, the seventh hour of the day, the third hour of the evening, the moment of the sun rising entire, a named constellation, and the moon in Leo — and the condition is in every case the date and never a state of either party, which is the first half of the statement. The second half it does not supply: it states no consequence for acting outside any of the nine windows. The witness therefore confirms the antecedent nine times over and leaves the consequent unattested, which is worth entering because the statement is a conditional and a case that meets the antecedent without meeting the consequent is where it would break. It does not break here — the statement claims only that where such a consequence is stated it lies in the time — but the case shows how much of the class is silent."@en,
        "Both new witnesses carry the first clause and neither carries the second, so they change the count and not the balance: the handbook names the day and the quarter and says nothing about what befalls an act done outside them, and the Herbarium does the same. The holding therefore has four witnesses for how the condition is stated and two for where the failure is located, and the second clause still rests on the Chinese material alone."@en,
        "Falsified by a transmission of this kind whose earliest witness names a compensating act — a substitute rite, a correction, a second attempt — that restores the effect for an operation performed outside the window, or one that makes the window itself depend on a state of the operator, since either would put the decision back into a party's hands. Weakened, without being refuted, if the windows of two such transmissions turn out to be derived from one another rather than arrived at separately; the two here are both Chinese and a route between them is not excluded, which is why the statement claims a recurring form and not an independent attestation."@en,
        "It narrows the statement and does not refute it, and the difference is worth being exact about. The chapter does not say that an operation performed outside its window works; it says that a man of this attainment no longer chooses days at all, which is a claim about who stands under the rule. Had it said that the practice takes effect in the hours of dead qi for such a man, the first clause would be gone."@en,
        "One of the two also marks a limit that the earlier witnesses did not show, and it is recorded here rather than left for a reader to notice. The handbook's slip against the falling sickness carries, in the same heading, a condition on the sufferer: the remedy is for one who has not yet fallen into fire or water. A calendar condition and a person-condition therefore stand on one prescription. That does not touch the statement, which says how a calendar condition is stated and not that a transmission may state nothing else; but it shows that the two kinds are not alternatives in practice, and a later run that reads the first clause as an exclusion of person-conditions would be reading it too widely."@en,
        "That narrowness is the reason for the two witnesses added on 2026-09-13, and they are the first from outside the Chinese sphere. The European folk-magic handbook binds a whole class of its acts to a named weekday, an hour before sunrise and a named quarter of the moon — Friday before sunrise in the waning moon for the knotting of twigs, the first Friday in the new light for the slip against the falling sickness, the last Friday in the old light for the letter against rheumatism. The Old English Herbarium binds one remedy for the same disease to two calendar conditions at once, the waning moon and the sun in the Virgin, which the text itself resolves into the month of August. Both were read at the wording; the second was found by a counter-search that was looking for something else. The spread is now three transmissions and two spheres between which no route has been proposed."@en,
        "The counter-search was run against the compiled holding before this was written, for transmissions that bind an operation to a time and for transmissions that refuse to. Nothing in the holding says of an operation of this kind that it works whatever the day. What the search did turn up is the case that keeps the second clause from being stated more strongly: the operative transmission of the science of letters prescribes seven days of the lunation on which its orations are to be said, and the holding already carries that at prescribed order rather than at stated necessity, on the ground that the text does not say what follows if a day is missed. A window with no stated failure is therefore a real and already recorded shape, and the statement is worded so that such a case does not falsify it."@en,
        "The finding is also a lesson about the counter-search this class requires. It ran against the compiled holding, as SPEC §16 prescribes, and came back empty; the case that limits the claim was in the unread part of a work already cited three times in this graph. Against a generalization drawn from two or three witnesses, the cheapest remaining counter-search is not another query but reading the rest of the works the witnesses come from. See prima-materia#674."@en,
        "The nearest counter-case was found on the day this node was written, and it was not in the holding at the time of the counter-search. It stands in the eighteenth chapter of the work whose eighth chapter supplies the first witness: one who can guard the One, it says, need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars, does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate, and in the end never meets with calamity. The exemption is granted by a state of the operator, which is the thing the first clause says a calendar-bound condition is not stated in terms of. It is what removed the word alone from that clause, and it is the reason the clause now says how the condition is stated rather than what it depends on."@en,
        "The one thing the search found on the other side is not a transmission but a critic. The seventh-century memorial on the burial books argues that the rites fix the interval between death and burial by rank, so that no year or month is chosen, and counts burials in the classical annals on the day the books call worst of all. That bears on whether the second witness is right and not on what it claims, and it is held where it belongs, at the dispute node beside the burial book."@en,
        "The shape of these claims is worth stating beside the claim, because it runs in two directions at once and this is the class in which both are visible in one sentence. A condition fixed by a calendar is the most examinable kind this holding carries: it is public, it is settled before the case, and no party to the operation can adjust it afterwards. The same condition is also the cheapest available defence, because any session, burial or needling that produced nothing can be assigned to the hour after the fact. Which of the two a given transmission intends is not decided here and probably cannot be decided from the texts."@en,
        "The three witnesses. The Baopuzi says that the circulation of the qi is to be done in the hours of living qi and not in the hours of dead qi, and that in the hours of dead qi it is of no benefit; the boundary is midnight and noon and nothing else enters it. The burial book names as the second of six adversities a going wrong of the year and the season, which makes the burial adverse however favourable the ground and however correct the rite. The Lingshu binds five needling operations to the stems of the day: on jia and yi days not the head, on bing and ding days not shoulder and throat, and so through the cycle."@en,
        "The two clauses of the statement are not equally carried, and the division is the useful part. All three witnesses carry the first: the condition is a date, and no examination of anybody could establish or remove it. Only two carry the second, because the Lingshu names the days and the places and stops, without saying what befalls one needled at the head on a jia day. A fourth place in the same corpus, the twelve mis-needlings by season, does state consequences, which suggests the silence belongs to that chapter rather than to the transmission; that is a reason to look further and not a reason to count it."@en,
        "Two of the three witnesses stand in the same tradition. The spread is therefore two transmissions and not three, and the claim is correspondingly weaker than the count of predecessors suggests. See prima-materia#666."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicAsterionPresupposesFenster,
        pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster,
        pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna,
        pmc:SanjiLiuxiong,
        pmc:ShangqingJinri,
        pmc:ShengqiSiqi,
        pmc:Wujin ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Transmissions that bind an operation to a calendar state the condition as a date and not as a state of the operator or of the person operated on; and where such a transmission states a consequence for acting outside the window, it locates the failure in the time rather than in either party."@en .

pmc:AegyptiaAhi a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ahi"@de,
        "Ahi"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 36, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Ahi, a sistrum bearer" ;
    skos:definition "The thirty-sixth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name sistrum bearer. The god is addressed as coming forth from Nu, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not made my voice haughty."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "ahi" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAmBesek a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Am-besek"@de,
        "Am-besek"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 16, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Am-besku, Eater of Livers, or intestines" ;
    skos:altLabel "Am-besku (Vocabulary, 1911)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The sixteenth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name eater of intestines. The god is addressed as coming forth from Mabet, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not desolated ploughed lands."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "am-besek" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAmKhaibit a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Am-khaibit"@de,
        "Am-khaibit"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 4, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Am-khaibitu, p. 82" ;
    skos:altLabel "Am-khaibitu (Vocabulary, 1911)"@en ;
    skos:definition "The fourth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name eater of shades. The god is addressed as coming forth from Qernet, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not slain men, twice said."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "am-khaibit" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAmSnef a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Am-snef"@de,
        "Am-snef"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 15, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. Am-snef, Eater of Blood" ;
    skos:definition "The fifteenth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name eater of blood. The god is addressed as coming forth from nemmat, the block, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not acted deceitfully."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "am-snef" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAnAF a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "An-a-f"@de,
        "An-a-f"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 41, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. An-a-f, p. 46" ;
    skos:definition "The forty-first address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name bringer of his arm. The god is addressed as coming forth from Maat, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not carried off the food of the infant, I have not sinned against the god of my town."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "an-a-f" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAnHetep a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "An-hetep"@de,
        "An-hetep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 125, the address to the forty-two gods, address 28, Papyrus of Ani, in the transliteration and English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London: British Museum, 1895, plates XXXI-XXXII, pp. 198-203",
        "Budge, A Hieroglyphic Vocabulary to the Theban Recension of the Book of the Dead, London: Kegan Paul, 1911, s.v. An-hetep, p. 48" ;
    skos:definition "The twenty-eighth address of chapter 125 in the Papyrus of Ani. Budge renders the name bringer of his offering. The god is addressed as coming forth from Sau, and the address is bound to one denial: I have not acted insolently."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:nameForm "an-hetep" ;
    pm:nameRole "one of the forty-two gods of the hall of the two Maati" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaArcanumAmenti a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Vollziehe dies vor keinem Menschen, denn es ist ein überaus großes Geheimnis"@de,
        "Perform this before no human being, because it is an exceedingly great mystery"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Book of the Dead, chapter 137A, rubric, Papyrus of Nu, in the English of E. A. Wallis Budge, London 1901, vol. II, pp. 417-418",
        "Book of the Dead, chapter 161, rubric, from the Turin text, in the English of Peter le Page Renouf and Édouard Naville, The Egyptian Book of the Dead, London: Society of Biblical Archaeology, 1904" ;
    skos:definition "The secrecy clause of the same rubric: thou shalt not perform this ceremony before any human being except thine own self, or thy father, or thy son, because it is an exceedingly great mystery of Amentet and is a type of the hidden things of the underworld. The rubric adds that these things shall be done secretly in the underworld, and that they are mysteries of Neter-khert."@en ;
    skos:note "No consequence is stated for breaking the clause in either translation. That is the ordinary state of this corpus and is recorded rather than filled in."@en,
        "The exception is the finding, and it is edition-dependent. Budge admits the father and the son to the ceremony; Renouf, translating the Turin text under chapter 161, has the same clause exclude them by name — reveal it to no one, may he be thy father or thy son, except thyself. The two readings put the boundary of the transmission in different places, one at the lineage and one at the single person, and no reading of the Egyptian was available to this run to decide between them. The node therefore reports both and asserts neither."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:AegyptiaQuattuorFlammae ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:BookOfTheDead ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EgyptianRitual .

pmc:AegyptiaAriEmAbF a pm:Naming ;
