pmc:AffectCouplingProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die Verschiebung eintritt, und ob die Behauptung etwas über sie sagt"@de,
        "Whether the shift occurs, and whether the claim says anything about it"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether a strong affect shifts attention, readiness and the threshold for risk, and of what remains of the claim once it does."@en ;
    skos:note "A convergence worth following separately. Several transmissions warn against exactly this coupling on the ground that it deceives the practitioner about his own position, and traditions/patanjala-yoga.ttl already carries pmc:SiddhisAsObstacle in that shape. If the direction of the shift is unknowable in advance, the warning and the finding say something close to the same thing from opposite ends, and that is a candidate for a convergence node rather than a remark here."@en,
        "Counter-search, carried and not favourable to either side. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, while the association between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The opposition of directions survives; its symmetry does not."@en,
        "On the state. This protocol is the case that made pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate necessary, and it is deliberately not recorded as cases without deviation. There the claim says something and the cases bear it out; here they bear it out because the claim does not say enough to be borne out against anything. Filing it as an ordinary confirmation would have added a success to a record that had in fact found the claim empty."@en,
        "That the shift occurs is not in doubt and the claim is borne out in that narrow sense. Strong affect measurably moves risk judgement and the choices that follow from it, in induced and in naturally occurring form alike."@en,
        "The consequence for anyone acting on the claim, which is the reason to enter this node at all. Whoever couples an intended state to a strong affect in order to shift his own threshold does not know from the claim whether he has raised or lowered it. Confirmation at this level of generality is not usable guidance, and the node says so rather than recording a success."@en,
        "What the evidence takes away in the same motion. The direction of the shift is set by the particular affect and not by its strength. Fear and anger are both strong and both unpleasant, and they move the threshold for risk in opposite directions: the fearful judge risk pessimistically and choose cautiously, the angry judge it optimistically and choose boldly, with the angry closer to the cheerful than to the fearful. A claim that names only the strength of the affect therefore cannot say which way anything moves, and is compatible with every outcome."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Carried. A registered replication of the key studies in a large online sample supports the framework only in part: the association between the emboldening affect and both risk-seeking and optimistic estimates holds, the one between the cautioning affect and risk-seeking does not reliably appear. The finding is in the notes above. Not searched: later meta-analytic work on the framework as a whole."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Beyond valence: toward a model of emotion-specific influences on judgement and choice, Cognition and Emotion 14 (2000), no. 4, pp. 473-493",
        "J. S. Lerner and D. Keltner, Fear, anger, and risk, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 81 (2001), no. 1, pp. 146-159, doi:10.1037/0022-3514.81.1.146" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The protocol reads the finding off experimental work on specific emotions and their appraisal tendencies, and off a registered replication of it" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Strong affect, induced or occurring, leaves risk judgement and the choices following from it unmoved against a neutral condition."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:AffectCouplingShiftsTheCarrier ;
    pm:undividedOutcomes "A threshold for risk that has been raised, and one that has been lowered. The claim covers both alike, so acting on it cannot tell the practitioner which of the two he has brought about."@en .

pmc:AhwalAreNotByEffort a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zustände werden nicht durch Anstrengung erlangt"@de,
        "The states are not attained by effort"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:notByEffort ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Ahwal ;
    pm:situationGround "The handbooks describe these as descending on the wayfarer as a gift and distinguish them from the stations precisely by their not being attainable through effort. That is the criterion this coordinate names, stated by the source itself."@en .

pmc:AhwalAreTransient a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zustände vergehen"@de,
        "The states pass"@en ;
    dcterms:source "al-Qushayrī, al-Risāla al-Qushayriyya" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:transientState ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Ahwal ;
    pm:situationGround "The same passage has them pass as well as descend, and the two are separate claims: a gift may be kept. The Sufi material answers both questions the same way, which is why one axis sufficed until a tradition answered them differently."@en .

pmc:AnapanasatiYieldsPiti a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Atemachtsamkeit bringt Verzückung hervor"@de,
        "Mindfulness of breathing yields rapture"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga IV.94ff. (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga VIII (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "Rapture appears in the handbook as a factor of absorption reached through the meditation subject, not as something aimed at directly. The pairing is recorded at that strength."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Anapanasati ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:Piti .

pmc:AnswersFromAnUncontrolledOutcome a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Antworten am Ausgang, den der Betreiber nicht beherrscht"@de,
        "Answers fixed to an outcome the operator does not control"@en ;
    skos:note "A third case was considered and not used. The Sámi drum answers by the travel of a ring that the operator does not steer, and the reading rule is attested twice ninety years apart (pmc:SunCourseRule). It is not entered here because that node states a rule of reading and not a claim that the reading comes true, and pm:generalizedFrom must point at claims and not at descriptions. Seen and left out, with the reason."@en,
        "The Zande transmission has a feature that makes this generalization examinable rather than merely stateable: it prescribes a control, the second consultation that must reverse the first. It also names eight causes to which a failure of that control is attributed, so the condition never falls against the oracle itself. Both stand in traditions/azande.ttl, and both belong in any protocol devised against this node."@en,
        "The comparison class is the whole of the claim and is the part to attack. An oracle that answers yes or no is measured against one half; a system of two hundred and fifty-six figures against one two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth. A protocol that does not fix the admissible set before the consultation measures nothing, because the set can always be narrowed afterwards to whatever came true."@en,
        "Two attested claims from two transmissions with no route between them that this holding has established: the Zande poison oracle and the casting of the Ifá figures. The mechanism is the same in both and is the reason they are taken together — the answer is not read off the operator's judgement but off an outcome he does not steer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch,
        pmc:CastingYieldsForeknowledge ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Procedures that fix an answer to the outcome of a physical process the operator cannot control name the state of affairs later established more often than an answer drawn at random from the same set of admissible answers."@en .

pmc:ApanaForcedAgainstItsCourse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der abwärts gerichtete Wind wird mit Gewalt aufwärts getrieben"@de,
        "The downward wind driven upward by force"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā III.62 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "The wind whose course is downward is said to be made to go upward by force; the manual states the natural course and the reversal in the same verse."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the direct reversal of any scheme that assigns downward carriage to an operation in the lower abdomen. The manual has the abdominal operation carry upward, and has the operation at the throat press the other wind down. Both assignments are the opposite of the one the modern draft makes. See prima-materia#28."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ApatheiaPresupposesAscesis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Apatheia setzt asketische Arbeit voraus"@de,
        "Apatheia presupposes ascetic labour"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Evagrius Ponticus, Praktikos 81" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Apatheia is called the flower of ascetic labour and ascetic labour is defined as the keeping of the commandments, so that where the labour is absent the freedom from the passions does not appear at all rather than appearing in a lesser degree." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Apatheia ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Praktike ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:DesertMonasticism .

pmc:AppearancesAreSelfArising a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erscheinungen sind Selbst-Erscheinung"@de,
        "The appearances are self-arising"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Bar do thos grol (Karma Lingpa recension, 14th century; trans. Gyurme Dorje 2006), the intermediate state of reality" ;
    skos:definition "The teaching that the figures met in the intermediate state are appearances of the one who meets them and not independently existing beings, and that recognising them as such is what frees."@en ;
    skos:note "Tibetan rang snang, self-appearance. A dating warning belongs on this node: the work is traditionally ascribed to Padmasambhava in the eighth century but is a treasure text of the fourteenth, revealed by Karma Lingpa; the older ascription is not held in the scholarship. Note also the scope. The doctrine holds of all appearance in this tradition, waking included, and is therefore not a statement about the threshold state in particular."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:AraqielTeachesSignsOfTheEarth a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Araqiel offenbart die Zeichen der Erde"@de,
        "Araqiel discloses the signs of the earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfTheEarth ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Araqiel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:AristeasJourney a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seelenreise, Aristeas zugeschrieben"@de,
        "The soul-journey ascribed to Aristeas"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Herodotus, Histories IV.13-16",
        "Pliny, Naturalis Historia VII.174" ;
    skos:definition "A capacity ascribed to a figure who is not available to report it, in a historian's account of what was told of him."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SoulJourney ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:GraecoRoman .

pmc:ArmarosTeachesLoosingOfSpells a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Armaros offenbart das Lösen von Beschwörungen"@de,
        "Armaros discloses the loosing of spells"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:LoosingOfSpells ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Armaros ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:ArsNotoriaCrimenKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wer durch ein Verbrechen behindert ist, darf sie nicht sprechen"@de,
        "No one impeded by crime may presume to say it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that no man impeded or corrupted with any crime ought to presume to say the oration; on a day on which he is so impeded he may only call it to mind in his heart."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is the closest thing in this witness to a condition of failure, and it is worth naming what it does not do: it names who may not say the words, not what happens to one who says them anyway. The distinction decides whether the condition is examinable or absorbing, and the text leaves it on the safer side of that line."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ArsNotoriaOratio ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaHorae a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Tagesstunden und die Zahl der Wiederholungen"@de,
        "The hours of the day and the count of repetitions"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 13-14, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The division of the day the orations follow: once in the morning, once about the third hour, once about the ninth hour and once at evening; where a great work presses, twice in the morning and twice about the ninth hour."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaPresupposesLunationes a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirkung setzt den bestimmten Tag voraus"@de,
        "The effect presupposes the appointed day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 14, p. 12, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The text says the orations are to be pronounced at certain determinate times of the moon and are not to be undertaken without consideration of the end; it does not say what follows if the day is missed. The strength is set to what the passage carries and no higher."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ArsNotoriaLunationes ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaPresupposesSolitudo a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Wirkung setzt Alleinsein und die gesenkte Stimme voraus"@de,
        "The effect presupposes solitude and the lowered voice"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), p. 14, ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ArsNotoriaFacundia ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ArsNotoriaSolitudo ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaRepetitioYieldsGradatio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die wiederholte Oration bringt Beredsamkeit nach Maß hervor"@de,
        "The repeated oration yields eloquence by measure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), pp. 46-47, ed. Peterson" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ArsNotoriaOratioRepetita ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ArsNotoriaGradatioFacundiae .

pmc:ArsNotoriaTerminusMortisDisputatus a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Tod auf den folgenden Tag oder nur auf bald gesetzt ist"@de,
        "Whether the death is set for the morrow or only soon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30a, p. 25, ed. Peterson",
        "Ars notoria, the Latin of the Beringos printing appended to the Agrippa Opera, § 30a, printed beside the translation in the edition of Peterson" ;
    skos:note "Held as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would need the medieval recensions, which were not opened; that the two readings stand side by side in one edition is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either."@en,
        "The Latin of the printing Turner worked from says of the sick man who answers badly that he is proculdubio in proximo moriturum, that is that he will die shortly, with no day named. Turner's English says: then doubtless conclude, He will dye on the morrow. Both stand in the edition read, on facing sides of the same passage."@en,
        "The difference falls on the only part of the claim that could be missed. A death that must come tomorrow can be watched for and can fail to arrive; a death that will come shortly has no last day, and no outcome ever refutes it. The English is therefore the examinable version and the Latin is not, and a protocol has to say which of the two it tests."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ArsNotoriaInterrogatioAegroti ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:ArsNotoriaVirtusNonExNatura a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Wunder geht nicht aus deiner eigenen Natur hervor"@de,
        "The miracle proceeds not from your own nature"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ars Notoria, trans. Robert Turner (London, 1657), § 30b, p. 25, ed. Peterson" ;
    skos:definition "The statement the text makes about its own procedure: that this miracle proceeds not from the operator's nature but from the nature and virtues of the holy angels, it being a part of their office wonderfully to reveal these things to him."@en ;
    skos:note "A transmission that says outright where it locates the acting is uncommon, and this one says it in the middle of an instruction rather than in a prologue. It is why the placement on the agency axis beside this file rests on the wording and not on a reading of it."@en ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ArsNotoria ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ArsNotoria .

pmc:AscentPresupposesTheRungs a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Schau der Schönheit selbst setzt den Aufstieg über die Stufen voraus"@de,
        "The vision of beauty itself presupposes the ascent by rungs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Plato, Symposium 211c" ;
    skos:note "The word rendered as rungs stands in the base text at 211c, which is why this counts as a stated ordering and not as a modern reading of a sequence of paragraphs."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ascent is described as rightly conducted only when it proceeds as by rungs of a ladder, from one body to two, from bodies to pursuits, from pursuits to studies, and thence to beauty itself; the one who does not go this way does not arrive at the final vision." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:BeautyItself ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:BeautyOfOneBody ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Platonic .

pmc:AsipuAnointingYieldsProtection a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Salbung hält Schaden vom Beschwörer ab"@de,
        "The anointing keeps harm from the exorcist"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 30-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuRamanaPasasu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuMimmaLemnuLaTehi .

pmc:AsipuCautionAgainstUnprotectedApproach a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nicht ungeschützt zum Kranken gehen"@de,
        "Do not go to the patient unprotected"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 29-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:note "A caution that a transmission addresses to its own operator rather than to the person operated on, and one that names what it protects against by list. The list is the same list of demons the incantations of the series drive away from the patient, which makes the exorcist, for the length of the visit, a second patient."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:AsipuLemnuAnaAsipiTehu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuLearnsTheRitesByDivineDecision a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Götter beschlossen, dass er ihre Riten lernen solle"@de,
        "The gods decided that he should learn their rituals"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 12: 39 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim the twelfth tablet puts in the speaker's mouth: they decided about me that I should learn their rituals and their ways."@en ;
    skos:note "Acquisition and gift stand in one sentence and neither cancels the other: the learning is his and the decision that he should learn is not. That is a shape the holding's axes cannot yet place, because the acquisition axis asks whether the source presents the state as earned or as given and this source presents it as both, at two different levels. The node is entered without an axis placement rather than forced onto one; no placement is a different record from a placement on the not-stated value, and here the source does state something."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuMashultuppuYieldsRemoval a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Sündenbock trägt das Übel fort"@de,
        "The scapegoat carries the evil away"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 12: 77-112 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuMashultuppu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuLemnuIntoTheNetherworld .

pmc:AsipuProvisionedDismissalYieldsDeparture a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die mit Zehrung versehene Entlassung schickt die Geister in die Steppe"@de,
        "The provisioned dismissal sends the ghosts to the steppe"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Udug-hul 7: 152-156 (canonical Utukku Lemnutu; critical edition M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsipuNaruqqaLeqe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsipuLemnuAnaSerika .

pmc:AsipuSelfProtectionBeforeApproach a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Salbung geht dem Herantreten voraus"@de,
        "The anointing comes before the approach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) rev. 29-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, p. 40)" ;
    skos:note "The strength is set at stated necessity and the reason for the doubt is recorded rather than hidden. The text gives a purpose clause and not a threat: anoint yourself, so that nothing bad will approach you. The consequence of omission is therefore the contrapositive of what stands written and not a sentence of the transmission, and a later reader who holds that this is one step short of a stated necessity would be arguing about the same words and not about a missing reading. What tips it is the rubric immediately above, which names the harm by list and by name; a purpose clause backed by a named harm says what is at stake if the step is left out."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ritual states the anointing as what secures that nothing bad approaches the exorcist when he approaches the patient; what is claimed for the omission is therefore that the demons of the rubric — Sheriff and Bailiff demon, epilepsy, the accessory to evil, the Alu-demon, the falling illness — approach him instead."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AsipuAnaMarsiTehu ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AsipuRamanaPasasu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsipuSpellOnSelfBeforeApproach a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Selbstbeschwörung geht dem Herantreten voraus"@de,
        "The spell on oneself comes before the approach"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sakikku (diagnostic series) Tablet 2, colophon (R. Labat, Traite akkadien de diagnostics et pronostics medicaux, Paris and Leiden 1951, pp. 18-19 n. 27)" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, although the wording is the sharper of the two: it is a prohibition and not a recommendation. What it lacks is the consequence. The colophon says you should not approach, and says nothing about what befalls the one who does; the ritual of the Assur tablet, which is weaker in form, is the one that names the harm. The pair is worth keeping side by side for that reason, because it shows that the firmness of a wording and the presence of a stated consequence vary independently in one and the same craft."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AsipuAnaMarsiTehu ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AsipuSiptaAnaRamaniNadu ;
    pm:readVia "M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons: Canonical Udug-hul Incantations (BAM 8, Boston/Berlin: De Gruyter 2016), p. 40" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsiputuLamaduYieldsIsipputu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Studium bringt die Beherrschung des Reinigungskorpus hervor"@de,
        "The study yields mastery of the corpus of purification"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 36 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsiputuLamadu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsiputuIskaruMasmassuti .

pmc:AsiputuMasteryBeforeJudgement a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Korpus wird beherrscht, bevor die Ansichten gewogen werden"@de,
        "The corpus is mastered before the views are weighed"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 36-37 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    skos:note "The order is given by the word afterwards standing at the head of the second stage, and by the clause until you master the entire corpus closing the first. No consequence is stated for taking the second stage first, so the strength stays at prescribed order; what the manual does state, at the end, is the reward for completing both, namely that the protective deity establish the student and that his name be mentioned forever."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:AsiputuSitulti ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:AsiputuLamadu ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:AsiputuSitultiYieldsMithurtu a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die zweite Stufe bringt das Wägen widerstreitender Ansichten hervor"@de,
        "The second stage yields the weighing of conflicting views"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Exorcist's Manual, KAR 44: 37-40 (Geller, in Steinert (ed.), Assyrian and Babylonian Scholarly Text Catalogues, BAM 9, De Gruyter 2018, p. 301)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:AsiputuSitulti ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AsiputuMithurtu .

pmc:AtharvanMedha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Weisheit, zuerst gerufen und gebeten einzutreten"@de,
        "Wisdom called first, and asked to enter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 6.108 verses 1 to 4, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 7, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "A short hymn for wisdom. Do thou, O wisdom, come first to us, with kine, with horses, thou with the sun's rays; I call first, unto the aid of the gods, wisdom filled with brahman, quickened by brahman, praised by seers, drunk of by Vedic students; the wisdom that the Ṛbhus know, that the Asuras know, the excellent wisdom that the seers know — that do we cause to enter into me; and, with that wisdom do thou make me today, O Agni, possessed of wisdom."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a capacity claimed and not as a claim that a practice produces it, and the distinction is the reason there is no yielding node here. The hymn is a petition throughout: wisdom is called, invited and asked to enter, and the one operative clause names Agni as the agent. What the manual makes of it — its use in the rite for begetting wisdom and at the initiation — was not read in the Sanskrit and is not entered."@en,
        "Whitney records that the Paippalāda has only verses 1, 2 and 5 of this hymn. The verse carrying the three classes who know wisdom is therefore transmitted in one recension of the two."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:AtrarpePatraic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Patrick verwarf sie, und wer sie übt, hat weder Himmel noch Erde"@de,
        "Patrick abolished these, and whoever practises them has neither heaven nor earth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Prologue to the Senchas Már, in Ancient Laws of Ireland, vol. I (Dublin: HMSO, 1865), p. 45",
        "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, English in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 95" ;
    skos:definition "The sentence the glossary appends to the procedure. Patrick abolished this and the teinm laegda, and he adjudged that whoever should practise them should have neither heaven nor earth, because it was renouncing baptism. The law prologue states the same abolition of the three things among the poets when they believed, as they were profane rites, and gives the ground that teinm laegha and imus forosna could not be performed without offering to idol-gods."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated and it falls on the operator: neither heaven nor earth. It is a consequence of performing and not of performing badly, which is the opposite direction from every other stated consequence in this file."@en,
        "The ground is the offering and never the outcome. Neither witness says that the procedures do not work, that they had failed, or that what they revealed was false; both say that they cannot be performed without an offering to demons and that the offering is what is forbidden. A prohibition of that shape is an attestation of efficacy by the prohibiting party, and the search that establishes there is no denial anywhere in what was read stands at pmp:CelticEfficacyDenialSearch."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Erlangung erlassen darf, ist die außerhalb des Übenden festgesetzte Bedingung"@de,
        "What attainment is allowed to waive is the condition fixed outside the practitioner"@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a passage in any operative transmission granting an experienced practitioner relief from a bodily or conduct condition — a fast, a term of continence, a washing, a dietary rule — on the ground of his attainment, stated as a waiver of a rule the same text imposes on others. One such passage and the statement is gone. It is deliberately easy to break, because the interest of the statement is entirely in the asymmetry: a transmission that calls its astrology a pedagogy for beginners and will not say the same of its fasting is telling a reader which of the two it believes is doing the work."@en,
        "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, for waivers granted on attainment and for any case in which the thing waived is a rule of the body. The nearest case found does not refute the statement and does bound it: the Mahānirvāṇa Tantra divides practitioners into the bound, the heroic and the divine and makes the division govern which rites a person is competent for, so that the bound one may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman while the heroic one performs a rite with wine, meat, fish, grain and union. That is not one practitioner released from a condition the transmission sets for all; it is a transmission that sets different conditions for different classes from the outset, and the wording above says relieves of one of its own prescribed conditions in order to keep the two apart. Whether the distinction holds under pressure is the first thing a later run should attack."@en,
        "The third witness is the negative half and it is the one that makes the statement more than a coincidence of two. The Zhen'gao states the loss from emission in a countable form and adds that even a person entered in the jade registers is not thereby exempt. There the question of a waiver for the advanced is raised explicitly, about a bodily condition, and answered no."@en,
        "The witnesses. The Key of Solomon says that so exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art, and gives as the ground that beginners lack faith in the experiments; it waives nothing else, and a search of both books for a corresponding relief from the nine days, the fast, the bath or the confession came back empty. The eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi says that one who can guard the One need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars, and does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate. Both waivers are granted for attainment and both fall on the calendar."@en,
        "What it does not say. It does not say the waived condition is idle, nor that the unwaived one is effective; both are claims about efficacy and this is a claim about how transmissions talk. And the count is two positive witnesses and one negative, which is thin. The two are separated by nine centuries and by any route anyone has proposed, which is worth something; it is not worth a general law, and the statement is worded as a regularity in what gets waived and not as a rule about all transmissions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti,
        pmc:ShangqingYijieQingyao,
        pmc:ShouyiMianLiri ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission relieves a practitioner of one of its own prescribed conditions on the ground of what he has attained, the condition relieved is one fixed outside him — a day, an hour, a calendar, a star — and not one bearing on his body or his conduct. Where the same transmissions speak of a bodily condition and attainment together, they say that the attainment does not relieve it."@en .

pmc:Awenyddion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die awenyddion, befragt über einen zweifelhaften Ausgang"@de,
        "The awenyddion, consulted upon a doubtful event"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Giraldus Cambrensis, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, in The Historical Works of Giraldus Cambrensis, trans. Thomas Wright (London: Bohn, 1863), pp. 501-502" ;
    skos:altLabel "awenyddion"@en,
        "awenydhyon"@en ;
    skos:definition "What the Welsh archdeacon reports of them in 1194. When consulted upon any doubtful event they roar out violently, are rendered beside themselves, and become as it were possessed by a spirit. They do not deliver the answer in a connected manner, but the person who skilfully observes them will find, after many preambles and many incoherent though ornamented speeches, the desired explanation conveyed in some turn of a word. They are then roused from their ecstasy as from a deep sleep and compelled as it were by violence to return to their senses; they do not recover until violently shaken by other people; and they cannot remember the replies they have given. The gifts are usually conferred upon them in dreams: some seem to have sweet milk or honey poured on their lips, others that a written schedule is applied to their mouths, and on waking they publicly declare that they have received the gift."@en ;
    skos:note "Reported by an outsider who is a churchman and who supplies his own aetiology, deriving the gift from Trojan descent and comparing it to Calchas and Cassandra. The report is entered for what it describes and not for what it explains, and the mode is set accordingly."@en,
        "Three elements match the Irish procedure closely enough to be worth naming and not close enough to be joined: a state entered for a question put by someone else, a return from it compared to waking from deep sleep, and a second party who must shake the practitioner out of it. The differences are as sharp: nothing is chewed, nothing is offered, there is no term, and the gift is not acquired by a procedure at all but conferred in a dream."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:AwenyddionAithchuinge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zweimal zum selben Punkt befragt, antworten sie ganz verschieden"@de,
        "Consulted twice on the same point, they answer in wholly different terms"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Giraldus Cambrensis, Descriptio Kambriae I.16, in The Historical Works of Giraldus Cambrensis, trans. Thomas Wright (London: Bohn, 1863), pp. 501-502" ;
    skos:definition "The sentence the same chapter adds after the description: if consulted a second or third time upon the same point, they will make use of expressions totally different; perhaps they speak by the means of fanatic and ignorant spirits."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated failure of reproducibility, put by the reporter as an observation and not as a rule of the art, and it is the rarest sentence in this file. The repeated question is the cheapest test there is, somebody evidently ran it, and what came back is recorded as having come back different. The transmissions this holding carries mostly forbid the repeated question; here it was asked and answered, and the answer did not hold."@en,
        "It is an outsider's report and he attaches his own explanation to it, that they may speak by ignorant spirits. The explanation is his; the result of the repetition is what the node holds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

