pmc:BadMideCaution a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der böse Midē, der die Vermögen zum Schaden wendet"@de,
        "The bad Midē who turns the powers to harm"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 168-169" ;
    skos:definition "The society names a figure of its own kind who employs the powers for evil purposes, who can assume the form of any animal and in that shape destroy the life of his victim. The warning is not against an outsider but against a member who has passed the same degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition that catalogues capacities and warns against their use is the shape this collection meets in Pātañjala Yoga as well, and the two warnings are not the same warning. There the capacities are called obstacles to the goal of the practice; here they are called dangerous in the hands of a man who wants harm. One is a caution about the practitioner's direction, the other about his intention."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MideDistantTouch,
        pmc:MideSoulSeizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:BaopuziAgainstTheZhuangzi a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Laozi und Zhuangzi den Weg zu den Unsterblichen tragen"@de,
        "Whether the Laozi and the Zhuangzi carry the way to the transcendents"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 8 (Shizhi), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 8",
        "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210" ;
    skos:definition "The eighth chapter sets itself against the books this file also rests on. Of the five thousand words it says that they are general and cursory throughout and nowhere set out a matter from head to tail so that it could be taken up and followed, and that to recite the classic by heart without the essential way is labour thrown away. Of Wenzi, Zhuangzi and Yin Xi it says that their writing derives from the Yellow Lord and Laozi and follows their broad intent but never states the decisive word, and that where they level life and death, call them no different, treat being alive as a corvée and dying as a rest, they are already a hundred thousand million li away from the transcendents. Their parables it allows for small uses only."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a dispute inside one tradition and inside one file, and it is worth a node because the graph would otherwise carry the Zhuangzi passage and the Baopuzi passage side by side as though they belonged together. The most systematic practitioner in the corpus says they do not. Whoever builds a convergence on Daoist material has to say which of the two sides he is counting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:BaraqelTeachesAstrology a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel offenbart die Astrologie"@de,
        "Baraqel discloses astrology"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Ethiopic; Charles 1912)" ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:Astrology ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Baraqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BaraqelTeachesSignsOfLightning a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Baraqel offenbart die Zeichen der Blitze"@de,
        "Baraqel discloses the signs of the lightning flashes"@en ;
    dcterms:source "1 Enoch 8:3 (Aramaic/Greek reconstruction; Nickelsburg 2001)" ;
    skos:note "Stands in the position the Ethiopic fills with astrology."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:SignsOfLightning ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Baraqel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Enochic .

pmc:BarasnumExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Reinigung, die es schlimmer macht"@de,
        "The purification that makes it worse"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vendidad, fargard 9.47-49, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 4, 2nd ed. (Oxford: Clarendon, 1895)" ;
    skos:definition "A protocol taken from the transmission's own reasoning. The claim is not that an unqualified purification is ineffective but that it increases sickness and death in the world beyond what they were. That is a directional claim about an outcome outside the practitioner, and it separates two states of affairs that a later observer could tell apart."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases. No series has been collected, and none is available from within the transmission: the man who would provide the case is executed before the outcome could be recorded, which is itself a feature of the protocol worth stating rather than passing over."@en,
        "The transmission supplies the falsifying condition without meaning to. It could have said that the wrong rite simply does not cleanse, which nothing would contradict; instead it named an effect in the opposite direction from the one intended, and thereby excluded an outcome. Where a claim of failure predicts nothing, this one predicts an increase."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol stated from the witness; no case series has been collected" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A recorded series of purifications carried out by persons known to be unqualified, after which sickness and death in the affected place did not rise above the rate where the rite was performed correctly or not at all. The transmission asserts an increase, so a flat or falling rate falls due against it." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BarasnumCaution .

pmc:BengeAtFirstContact a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Giftprobe beim ersten dauerhaften Kontakt"@de,
        "The poison ordeal at the first sustained contact"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Georg Schweinfurth, The Heart of Africa, trans. Ellen E. Frewer (London: Sampson Low, Marston, Low and Searle, 1873), vol. II, chapter XIII, the account of the Niam-niam praying-machine and auguries" ;
    skos:definition "The earliest European account, from travels of eighteen sixty-eight to eighteen seventy-one, already describes the whole apparatus: an oily preparation from a red wood, given to a hen, whose death or survival decides the question; the rubbing-board consulted before any undertaking of consequence; and the use of both to establish the guilt or innocence of the accused, with suspected witches tried by the same ordeal. It records a case in which a hostile chief called off an attack because his fowl had died."@en ;
    skos:note "The corroborative second test is not in this account. That is an argument about the account and not about the transmission: the writer was a botanist passing through with an ivory company, not an observer of consultations, and he reports the outcome of single fowls because that is what he saw. What the silence does establish is that the double test is not attested before the monograph in anything read here, and the question of when it can first be shown is left open as an issue."@en,
        "The passage is the reason the contact route on this tradition is not simply documented. The oracles stand in the record at the first sustained contact with the Islamic north and cannot have come in with it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:BengeCorroborationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rate, mit der der Bestätigungstest scheitert"@de,
        "The rate at which the corroborative test fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "A count of the corroborated consultations printed in the source: how often the second test fell opposite to the first, as the transmission's rule of validity requires, and how often it fell alike and voided the verdict."@en ;
    skos:note "Of the two seances printed, the first carries two corroborated pairs and both are recorded as contradictory: a question about taking on the taboos of vengeance against a question about the questioner's own survival under them, and a question about a man's safety in a named settlement against its explicit corroboration. The second seance carries one explicitly corroborative question, and it agreed; the recorder calls that seance a wholly successful consultation. Three pairs, two of them void by the transmission's own rule."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The count is the recorder's material and the counting is this graph's. He states outright that the seances are given so that the reader may judge for himself the proportion of fowls that die and the order of deaths and survivals, so the arithmetic is invited by the source and is not imposed on it."@en,
        "The state is cases with deviation and not cases without, and the deviation is from the transmission's own stated requirement rather than from a base rate of chance. A base rate is deliberately not entered: the poison is administered in a number of doses that varies with the size of the fowl and with the operator's reading of its earlier answers, so the probability that a given fowl dies is not fixed and no honest chance rate can be stated from what was read."@en,
        "Three pairs decide nothing about a rate and the number is entered so that a later reader can see how small it is. What it does establish is that the falsifying condition is not hypothetical: it occurred in two of the three cases this graph has actually read, and the source says elsewhere that the oracle frequently kills or spares both fowls."@en,
        "What the deviation is not. It is not evidence against the oracle in the transmission's own terms, because the transmission assigns every occurrence of the condition to one of eight causes that leave the instrument untouched. That is why the finding of this protocol is about the standing of the falsifying condition inside the transmission and not about the oracle's accuracy, which nothing read here measures."@en ;
    pm:caseCount 3 ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination for why the undocumented case is treated as uncarried."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), the two seances printed at pp. 141-143" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "The Azande, by the corroborative second test their own rule prescribes; the two consultations counted here were recorded and printed at length by E. E. Evans-Pritchard, who was present at both" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "The two tests of one consultation fall alike, both fowls dying or both surviving, so that by the transmission's own rule the verdict is contradictory and void."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BengeYieldsIdentificationOfTheWitch .

pmc:BhastrikaPresupposesLevelNeckAndTrunk a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Blasebalgatem setzt einen gerade gehaltenen Hals und Rumpf voraus"@de,
        "The bellows breath presupposes neck and trunk held level"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.60 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:note "The verse binds the lotus seat, a level neck and trunk, and a carefully closed mouth into the one instruction that opens the bellows breath. It is the only place in the breath chapter where the alignment of the spine is stated as a condition of a breath practice, and it is stated for one practice, not for the chapter. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HathaPranayama ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ErectTrunkInSwastikasana ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:BhavaClassification a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die drei Dispositionen: paśu, vīra, divya"@de,
        "The three dispositions: paśu, vīra, divya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra I.50-57 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)",
        "Mahānirvāṇa Tantra IV.19-20 (trans. A. Avalon, Tantra of the Great Liberation, London 1913)" ;
    skos:definition "A division of practitioners into three kinds, the bound, the heroic and the divine, which governs the rites a person is competent for. The source describes the divine as all but a god, pure of heart, alike towards opposites and unattached, and the bound as one who must gather his own offerings and who may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the tradition's own answer to the question why one person takes one way and another another: not by choosing, but by what he is held competent for. The text presses the point polemically — it argues that if the bound disposition cannot exist in this age, the divine cannot either, and that only the heroic rites bear visible fruit. A record that reports the two paths as a choice between camps reports the later Western reading and not this one. See prima-materia#54."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:BiansuiDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob irgendein Ausgang gegen die Kunst zählen könnte"@de,
        "Whether any outcome could count against the art"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Wang Chong, Lunheng, the treatise on distinguishing calamities (Bian sui), juan 24, Sibu congkan facsimile",
        "Zangshu, miscellaneous section, in the recension of Wu Cheng, one juan, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "The same treatise sets out, in the chapter on distinguishing calamities, what happens to a counter-example put to a practitioner of these arts, and it does so by carrying one out three times. Suppose ten people in one household who move nothing, break no ground, change no dwelling, and choose auspicious days for every sacrifice, marriage and journey from spring to winter, offending against no prohibition: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? The diviner, the treatise says, will then answer that a dwelling has its flourishing and its decay, and that the year-breaker and the direct-symbol were not avoided. Suppose then that they consult the practitioners at each turn, stay where the dwelling flourishes and leave where it decays, and move the whole household whenever the year-breaker falls: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? He will then answer that moving offends against the time and that going and coming were not auspicious. Suppose they consult on that too: in a hundred years, can they avoid dying? He will then answer that the allotted span was simply at an end. The treatise concludes that on that showing life and death have their own term after all, and that no offence against year or month brought them about. It names the interest behind the regress in the same chapter: seeing a misfortune, the practitioners of the art point to the taboo and say nothing of the rest; hearing of a fortune, they keep it back and let it go unreported; they heap up misfortunes to alarm the careless and set out fortunes to hearten those who fear the times. And it puts three mass cases against the doctrine: the city of Liyang, which sank in one evening and became a lake, whose people had not all offended against the year and the month; the forty thousand of the Zhao army buried alive at Changping, who had not all left home at an unchosen hour; and the thousand prisons of the empire with ten thousand inmates, who had not all transgressed a taboo."@en ;
    skos:note "The second argument, about which cases the practitioners report, is a statement of selection in the record. It is the one argument in the treatise that a modern protocol could take over unchanged, and it is prior to any count: where only the confirmations are collected, a count of confirmations establishes nothing."@en,
        "The three mass cases are weaker than they look and the treatise does not notice why. Each says that a large group shared an outcome without sharing a cause, which refutes a doctrine claiming that the cause is necessary; the burial book does not claim that, and its annotation would be free to say that each of the drowned of Liyang had his own reason. The regress above is the stronger instrument precisely because it needs no case at all."@en,
        "This is the most valuable thing read in the treatise and the reason for opening it. It is a first-century description of an immunising regress, carried out step by step against a living practitioner, and it ends where such regresses end, at a clause that covers every remaining case. The graph holds the same machinery from the inside, at the six adversities of the burial book, whose annotation enumerates the ways in which nothing happens. Here it is described from the outside, six hundred years earlier, by someone who put it to a practitioner."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ZangYieldsYin ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination .

pmc:BirchBarkRecord a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Birkenrindenkarte"@de,
        "The birchbark chart"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Walter James Hoffman, The Midē'wiwin or Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology (Washington: Government Printing Office, 1891), pp. 164-165" ;
    skos:definition "The record the priests keep on birchbark, incised in fine lines, giving pictorially the ground plan of the degrees to which the owner is entitled."@en ;
    skos:note "Two things follow from this and both bear on how the rest of the file is to be read. The society keeps a written record by its own means, which is unusual among the transmissions this graph registers as lacking a corpus. And the capacities of the degrees are stated as readings of marks on such a chart, so what the report carries is an informant's exegesis of a pictograph and not a free verbal account; that is a different kind of witness, and arguably a firmer one, because the marks were there before the question was asked."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Midewiwin .

pmc:BothMethodsReachSiddhiForKamakhya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Für Kāmeśvarī und Kāmākhyā erreicht jede der beiden Methoden die Kraft"@de,
        "For Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā either method attains the power"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Kālikāpurāṇa, at the verses on worshipping Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā by either method (139b-140a) (text, introduction and translation in English by B. N. Shastri, part III, Nag Publishers, Delhi 1991)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that the goddesses Kāmeśvarī and Kāmākhyā may be worshipped at will following either the orthodox or the heterodox method, and that the doer attains siddhi doing it either way."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage that most directly rules out reading the pair as two camps. The choice is left to the worshipper, both methods reach the same attainment, and the text sees no need to rank them. Whatever the division does in this work, it does not sort people into parties and it does not make one method the true one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:BrowContactProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob der Ort der Berührung einen Unterschied macht"@de,
        "Whether the place of contact makes a difference"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether contact at the brow differs from contact elsewhere on the head in what the recipient reports."@en ;
    skos:note "Procedure without cases. The design is the same as for the palms and inherits the same controls, with one addition that matters more here: the brow is the place the recipient has most often been told about, so an effect found without blinding him to the site would be an effect of the telling."@en,
        "What a negative result would and would not settle. It would touch the draft's addition and leave pmc:Saktipata untouched: a descent of power that names no place cannot be refuted by showing that a named place does nothing. Keeping the two apart is why this node tests the posit and not the transmission."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The falsifying condition follows from the claim naming a site" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Recipients blinded to the site of contact report no difference between contact at the brow and contact at another site on the head beyond chance."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:BrowContactOpensTheBrow .

pmc:BudgeOrderingOfEthiopianAmulets a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Budges Ordnung der äthiopischen Amulette unter der Macht der Namen"@de,
        "Budge's ordering of the Ethiopian amulets under the power of names"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1900" ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Miracles of the Blessed Virgin Mary, and the Life of Hanna (Saint Anne), and the Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), introduction, pp. L-LIII" ;
    skos:definition "The account given in the introduction to the volume, in which the amulets are explained by a single principle: that wherever the name of a good or evil spirit, or of a man, existed, there also was the presence of that spirit or man, so that the presence of a name is equivalent to the presence of the being; and that the Ethiopians, having become Christians outwardly, retained their fear of demons and could resist one only by obtaining the protection of another of greater power."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an ordering act and not as a claim of the transmission because none of the rolls printed in the volume says any of it. The formula about the presence of the name is the editor's, it is the sentence most often repeated about this material, and it is regularly given as an Ethiopian doctrine."@en,
        "The same introduction summarises the sixth section of the roll written for Za-Walda Hawaryat as declaring that the man who wears this amulet on his breast shall be delivered from the spear of any who would attack him. The translation printed sixty pages later gives two forms, the spoken one first, and attaches the reservation by the will of God to the worn one only. A reader of the summary gets the guarantee without the reservation and without the alternative, which is the part of the sentence a protocol turns on."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "E. A. Wallis Budge" ;
    pm:ordersClaim pmc:EthiopianAsmat .

pmc:Camac a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Camac, der angerufene Macher"@de,
        "Camac, the maker that is invoked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V, the invocation of the huaca; cap. VII, the pacarina of each lineage; cap. XIV, the third question of the visitor's interrogatory" ;
    skos:definition "The name under which the power is addressed at the moment of asking. When they invoke the huaca they call it Runapcamac, maker of men, and ask it for life, health and food, and they ask it nothing for any other life. Each lineage names its own pacarina, the place it says it came out of, and calls that Camac, maker; and each says it has its own maker, one a hill, one a spring."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is entered at the strength the three passages give it and no higher. All three are the same recorder, and all three show him using the word as evidence that the Indians give to a stone what is due to God alone. What can be taken from them is that camac was the word used in address and that it was understood as making rather than as owning or protecting. What cannot be taken from them is a doctrine of camac as a substance or a force, which is a reading of the Quechua of Huarochirí and stands on a document this run could not open."@en,
        "The third of the three places is a question of the interrogatory the visitor was to put in every village. That the word had to be asked for by name shows that it was the recorder's own diagnostic and not an incidental report, which strengthens the evidence that it was in use and weakens the evidence about what it meant."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CatequillaPrediction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Huaca, die einen Tod voraussagte und dafür verbrannt wurde"@de,
        "The huaca that foretold a death and was burned for it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. II, on the idol Catequilla" ;
    skos:definition "A famous idol of one province was consulted through its priests by an Inca passing with an army, about whether he would return victorious from a battle or die in it; the answer was that he would die, and so it fell out. From this the huaca came to have so great a name that people came from very remote provinces to consult it and to make offerings, and it grew rich and had a sumptuous temple. His son, passing later and seeing that greatness, and learning that it was the huaca which had announced his father's death, ordered the temple and all in it to be burned; the priests stole the idol out of the fire and carried it elsewhere and built it another temple."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated hit, with its consequence stated too: the reputation of the huaca is derived from a single prediction that came true, by the recorder and presumably by the tradition he heard it from. It is the only place in this file where a claim about accuracy is made about a particular occasion rather than about a method."@en,
        "Entered as a claim standing in the work and not as a report of an event. Its chain is long — an extirpator writing in sixteen twenty-one about what another visitor was told about a consultation before the conquest — and no part of it was checked against another witness in this run."@en,
        "The second half is the more useful. The correct answer cost the huaca its temple, because the person it was correct about was the father of the man who burned it. A transmission in which an accurate oracle can be destroyed for its accuracy is not one in which reputation tracks accuracy, and the story says both things at once."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean .

pmc:CautionAgainstCarryingAnotherShamansSoul a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die letzte Überführung nicht im Zweifel übernehmen"@de,
        "Do not undertake the last transport in doubt"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, p. 382" ;
    skos:definition "When a shaman dies among the Northern Tungus his soul must be taken to the lower world by another shaman who is stronger than the deceased. The spirits left behind often want to keep the dead man in their company, so the one who performs the operation must first fight them. If he is not certain of being able to master the spirits of the deceased, he must not undertake it."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition addressed to the practitioner and conditioned on his own estimate of his strength, which is unusual: most cautions in this graph are addressed to the layman or to the beginner. If no sufficiently strong shaman exists, the soul is said not to reach the world of the dead and to disturb the clansmen without rest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:TungusShamanizing ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:CautionAgainstOperatingAcrossTheSexes a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Frauen sind von Frauen zu magnetisieren"@de,
        "Women are to be magnetized by women"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 107-108, and chap. VIII, pp. 161-164" ;
    skos:definition "Women ought to be preferred as operators upon women and, outside cases where plain good sense makes it indifferent, they alone ought to be charged with it; in chronic complaints requiring long treatment the practice between persons of different sexes is to be proscribed, the only men who may undertake the treatment of a young woman being her father or her husband. Four reasons are given: the processes wound decency; somnambulism generally imparts to the subject a lively affection for the operator which continues after the treatment ends; there are symptoms and questions about which a woman in that state will not speak to a man; and the crises are such that a man ought not to witness them nor could employ the processes best calculated to soothe them."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution of the ordinary kind, and it is entered for a reason that has nothing to do with propriety: it prescribes, for the great majority of the cases the practice treated, an operator drawn from the patient's own household and instructed by the patient's own book. The transmission thereby makes its typical operator a person with no independent means of judging the result, which bears on every case report in the literature."@en,
        "The same chapters apply the rule to the hospitals, where the source says young women and girls with nervous diseases were being chosen in preference because they were believed more susceptible and more likely to present curious phenomena. That sentence is a report about how the examinations of the day were in fact staffed, and it is the strongest thing in the file about the composition of the evidence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstOpinionAboutHiddenThings a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Warnung davor, sich über die verborgenen Dinge eine Meinung zu bilden"@de,
        "The caution against forming opinions about hidden things"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Rasmussen, Intellectual Culture of the Iglulik Eskimos, Report of the Fifth Thule Expedition 1921-24, VII.1, trans. W. Worster (Copenhagen: Gyldendalske Boghandel, Nordisk Forlag, 1929), pp. 19-20" ;
    skos:definition "The reported attitude that these are great and difficult questions best left alone, that men know so little beyond food and sleep and rest that it might easily seem presumptuous to form any opinion about hidden things, and that people who are content should not trouble themselves with thinking."@en ;
    skos:note "A tradition warning against the very enquiry the rest of it makes possible. It is entered because a record that carries only the capacities and not the cautions misreports what it holds, and because this caution is directed at the enquirer and not at the practitioner: it does not say the hidden things are unreal, it says that opinion about them is presumption."@en,
        "The passage also gives a second and worldlier ground for the same reticence, which the ethnographer reports of himself and not of the tradition: that his hosts feared their séances and their taboo observances would be laughed at. Both grounds are in the same pages, and separating them is the reader's work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:InuitHiddenThings ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InuitAngakkuq .

pmc:CautionAgainstProlongedSomnambulisme a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Das somnambule Vermögen nach der Heilung nicht erhalten"@de,
        "Do not keep the somnambulic faculty after the cure"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 173-174" ;
    skos:definition "Persons long somnambulists retain, even after recovery, a nervous susceptibility so high that the slightest action may return them to an imperfect somnambulism, and some have been seen habitually in the magnetic state. The operator is therefore to operate no longer than the somnambulist says is necessary, never to speak to him after waking of what he said, to discharge the fluid and wake him perfectly at the end of each sitting so that no intermediate point remains, and as soon as the patient is cured to will that the faculties cease."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule closes the practice's one route to a body of repeatable observations, and the source says as much without drawing the consequence: many operators keep somnambulists after their cure hoping to serve other patients through them, and it is wrong to have confidence in such somnambulists, who are often used for experiments of curiosity. A transmission that dismisses its trained subjects on recovery cannot accumulate cases."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstQuestionsOutsideHealth a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Somnambulen nicht über seine eigene Gesundheit hinausschicken"@de,
        "Do not send the somnambulist beyond his own health"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VIII, pp. 174 and 185-188" ;
    skos:definition "The somnambulist is to be interdicted absolutely from all religious, metaphysical and political discussion. Where he is required to see the dead or spirits, compelled to go into distant times or places, to discover things lost, to announce the future, to name the numbers that will be fortunate in a lottery, or interrogated about political affairs, much mischief will be done him and he might even be made idiotic."@en ;
    skos:note "The named consequence is severe, and the list is worth reading as a list: every item on it is a claim that could be checked against an outside record, and the lottery number is the only one for which a plain arithmetic of success exists. The prohibition therefore falls, item by item, on exactly the class of claims that would have been decidable."@en,
        "The source's reason for the prohibition is not that the faculty fails there but that the somnambulist is harmed and led into error, and he grounds it in a disorder of proportion: one faculty is exalted more than the others, harmony no more exists, the prejudices of childhood operate afresh, and imagination can realise the phantoms it has created. That is a psychological account of false report offered by a believer, and it is the most acute thing in the chapter."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeLucidite ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstSeekingSomnambulisme a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Somnambulismus niemals herbeiführen wollen"@de,
        "Never seek to produce somnambulism"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 70-71" ;
    skos:definition "The first advice given: never seek to produce somnambulism, but let it come naturally in order to profit by it when it takes place. Operators who charge the head in order to produce it often succeed in obtaining a forced slumber, a reflux of blood towards the brain, and partial crises which are of no utility; and the method is not without danger."@en ;
    skos:note "A self-warning of the strongest kind — the transmission's own prohibition of the effect for which it is known, placed at the head of the chapter about that effect. Beside it stands the statement that somnambulism too much prolonged would of itself become a disease."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:MagnetismeGrandsCourans ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstSelfMagnetizing a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Wer den Zustand allein herbeiführen kann, soll es niemals tun"@de,
        "Those who can enter the state alone ought never to do it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. VI, pp. 112-113" ;
    skos:definition "Among persons often magnetized there are some who can of their own accord throw themselves into the magnetic state; the source holds it a faculty they ought never to employ, because by exercising it they acquire the habit of concentration, which may fatigue the nervous system and become very injurious. An operator in good health may relieve a local pain of his own by applying the processes to himself, but a person with a general disease, a fever or an organic affection cannot draw the remedy from himself, since the fluid he would use no longer has the necessary qualities."@en ;
    skos:note "Carried because it closes the last door by which a claim of this tradition could be checked cheaply and often. A subject who can enter the state alone could be examined without an operator present, which would remove the one figure whose disposition every excuse in this file appeals to; the transmission forbids it, on grounds of the subject's health."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstTheWrongPresence a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die falsche Anwesenheit zerstört die Kraft der Sache"@de,
        "The wrong presence destroys the power of the thing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Frances Densmore, Teton Sioux Music (Bureau of American Ethnology Bulletin 61, Washington, 1918), p. 252",
        "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 143 and p. 145 and p. 92" ;
    skos:definition "Both recorders report that a ceremony or a medicine is undone by who is present. The first states that no ceremony will be efficacious if a woman is present during her menstrual flow or if the influences that surround her at that time are present, that such influences remain about a tipi she has occupied until it is taken down and set up again, and that the conductor of the Buffalo ceremony walked the lodge scanning the women to find such a one and the men to find an unworthy one, and would have ordered either to withdraw. The second states that the presence of the wrong kind of person in the lodge was believed to affect the efficacy of a medicine, and that if it were exposed to such influence for any considerable time its power would be entirely destroyed."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is entered against the imparted potency rather than against any single rite, because that is the thing both sources say is destroyed: the first speaks of a ceremony that will not be efficacious, the second of a medicine whose power is entirely destroyed, and what the two have in common is the potency the rite is supposed to put to work."@en,
        "The measure the first source prescribes is worth recording exactly, because it is physical and datable: the tipi in which the young woman had lived alone was taken down by her mother and immediately set up again on the same spot, and that act is what made it fit for the ceremony."@en,
        "This is the failure condition that does the most work in the file, because it is prior to every other: it can be invoked after the fact against any rite that did not take, and it needs no evidence beyond the assertion that somebody was there. Entered as a caution and not as a mere doctrine because both sources give it in the imperative, with an action to be taken."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Sicun ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CautionAgainstTouchingTheBurialGear a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Was am Grab bleibt, nicht anrühren"@de,
        "Do not touch what is left at the grave"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXX, sect. 141, p. 382" ;
    skos:definition "Among the Tungus of Transbaikalia the shaman's paraphernalia are laid with the body or hung on a tree standing nearby. It is not recommended to touch them: those who do may get mental diseases, they may lose their minds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:TungusParaphernalia ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:CautionAgainstTrustingThePrescription a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Verordnung des Somnambulen nicht ungeprüft folgen"@de,
        "Do not follow the somnambulist's prescription without examining it"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. V, pp. 83-86, and chap. VIII, pp. 175-177 and 183-184" ;
    skos:definition "Against the blind confidence of enthusiastic operators, who believe their somnambulists infallible, suppose that remedies which did not succeed were not exactly followed, and regard remedies that did mischief as a necessary crisis. Where the prescription appears unsuitable the operator is to object, have the organs examined in succession, ask the reasons for the choice, present the medicine to be touched and tasted, and have the dose shown as a quantity and not only named; if after all that the somnambulist persists, he may be depended upon. Where the prescription would put life in imminent danger the operator is evidently not to conform to it."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution the transmission addresses to its own adherents, naming the two devices by which they absorb failure — non-compliance and the necessary crisis — and rejecting both. That is a transmission criticising the unfalsifiability of its own practitioners, and it is the reason this file does not read as apologetics."@en,
        "It stops short of the step that would matter. The prescriptions are to be submitted to a candid physician before being taken, and the physician is to preserve his supremacy — but he is not to be put in consultation with the somnambulist, and his judgement is therefore never recorded against the somnambulist's in a form anybody could count."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeAutoDiagnostic ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAgainstWakingSuddenly a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Kranken niemals plötzlich erwecken"@de,
        "Never wake the patient suddenly"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. III, pp. 48-49" ;
    skos:definition "The patient is never to be woken suddenly, nor anyone permitted to trouble him, nor is he to be left while the induced state lasts. Where a person in profound sleep is roused roughly the source has seen it produce convulsions or violent pains, render the return of somnambulism impossible, and so change the patient's condition that he could afterwards not endure the action at all, his restoration being left to time and regimen; persons put into somnambulism for the first time have been known to lose the faculty of entering it again by being rudely roused."@en ;
    skos:note "Named consequences of a rough waking, and among them one a bystander could establish: that the state cannot afterwards be induced in that person again. That is a prediction about a particular individual on a particular date and it is the kind of thing a register of cases would settle. None is kept."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:MagnetismeSomnambulisme ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:CautionAtrarpePatraic a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Gegen die beiden Verfahren, wegen des Opfers"@de,
        "Against the two procedures, on the ground of the offering"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, and in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 95" ;
    skos:note "A warning transmitted in the same entry as the prescription it forbids, by the hand that copied the prescription. The glossary sets out the procedure in full and in the present tense and then abolishes it, and neither the setting out nor the abolition is abridged for the other's sake."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ImbasForosnai ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:CautionOnTheCandidatesConduct a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die acht Regeln, die einen Kandidaten binden"@de,
        "The eight rules that govern a candidate"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 71 and p. 93" ;
    skos:definition "The invariable rules taught to a candidate for the second, third or fourth form: he must subordinate himself to his mentor, meditate continually on his undertaking, speak little with others than his mentor, and use only his consecrated implements and utensils; he must not become angry, must not hear ribald speech, must not go into the water, and must not have sexual intercourse. A candidate who disregards any of them must do such penance as his mentor prescribes before he can proceed."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning with a remedy rather than a forfeit: the breach does not void the undertaking, it interrupts it until a penance set by another man has been done. That is a different shape from the taboos which are said to destroy an instrument outright, and the two stand in the same corpus."@en,
        "Two further restrictions belong to the same regime and are entered here rather than as separate nodes: the consecrated clothing, implements and utensils may be used by no one else until the dance is danced, and from the beginning of his last day in the sacred lodge the candidate must fast and take no drink until he has danced. Only a shaman can release him from any requirement of his conduct in the sacred lodge."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:WiwanyangWacipi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CedarWardsTheWingedGod a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zedernholz hält den Geflügelten Gott fern"@de,
        "Cedar keeps the Winged God away"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 70 and p. 110 and p. 133" ;
    skos:definition "The rack set at the foot of the candidate's bed of sage is of cedar because the cedar is favoured by the Winged God, who will not visit one protected by it nor cause such a one to act foolishly. Cedar leaves and twigs are also sprinkled over the altar in the dance lodge, where they are said to ward against the contrary conduct of the Winged God and of the heyoka."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is worth holding because it is the counter-move to the obligation on the neighbouring node: the same transmission that says a communication can bind a man for life also names a material that prevents it. A third means is named in the Hunka ceremony, where the waved wands are said to shield a man from seeing the person of the Winged God and so from being made heyoka. Three protections against one outcome, from three different rites, all in the same paper."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CentumAutumni a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die hundert Herbste und das Maß, das vor ihnen nicht wieder genommen werden darf"@de,
        "The hundred autumns, and the measure that may not be taken again before them"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Atharvaveda Saṃhitā 19.67 and 18.2 verse 38, in the translation of W. D. Whitney, revised by C. R. Lanman, Harvard Oriental Series 8, Cambridge, Mass. 1905" ;
    skos:definition "The term this corpus sets on a human life, stated twice in the hymns opened and in two different postures. As a petition, in eight one-line verses: may we see a hundred autumns, may we live, may we wake, may we ascend, may we prosper, may we be, may we adorn a hundred autumns, more autumns than a hundred. And as a stipulation at a funeral, where a measure is drawn on the ground: this measure do we measure, so that one may not measure further; in a hundred autumns, not before."@en ;
    skos:note "The second passage is a term with a lower bound and is the more interesting of the two. It does not ask for a hundred autumns; it fixes a hundred autumns as the interval within which the act may not be repeated, which makes the number a rule about the living rather than a wish for them."@en,
        "Whitney records that the hymn of the hundred autumns is not found in the Paippalāda at all, and that its first two lines are the last two padas of a Rigvedic verse which several later manuals extend differently. The formula is old and the hymn as a hymn is one recension's."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Atharvavedic .

pmc:Chilan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chilan"@de,
        "Chilan"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Diego de Landa, Yucatan Before and After the Conquest, translated by William Gates (Baltimore: The Maya Society, publication 20, 1937), section XXVII, pp. 46-47" ;
    skos:altLabel "chilam"@en,
        "chilanes"@es ;
    skos:definition "The office of the speaker who gave out the oracles to those of the locality. The witness reports that the respect shown them was so great that they did not ordinarily leave their houses except carried on litters."@en ;
    skos:note "The same section gives the office of the priest beside it: to teach the sciences, to indicate calamities and the means of remedying them, to preach at the festivals and to celebrate the sacrifices. Indicating a calamity and naming its remedy are one office in this transmission, which is why the remedy for a foretold year below is not a separate institution."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MayaRitual .

pmc:ChimaiGuanTongzi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rote Gefäße über der Pupille, nach Jahren gezählt"@de,
        "Red vessels crossing the pupil, counted for years"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 70 (Han re), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 10",
        "Huangdi neijing lingshu 74 (Lun ji zhen chi), in Lingshu jing, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 11" ;
    skos:definition "Asked how life and death are decided in the scrofulous cold-and-heat swellings, the chapter says to turn the eye back and look: where there are red vessels within it running from above down across the pupil, one vessel means death in a year, one vessel and a half a year and a half, two vessels two years, two and a half two and a half, three vessels three years. And where red vessels are seen that do not run down across the pupil, the case can be treated."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the reason this is the most examinable claim read in this corpus. The prognosis names the finding under which it predicts nothing, which is what a claim must do to be able to fail; and the finding itself is visible on a living eye, countable, and prior to the outcome by a year or more. Almost nothing else here has all three."@en,
        "The passage stands twice in the Lingshu, and the doublet is not verbatim. In the chapter on cold and heat the whole rule is given, including the clause about the vessels that do not cross; in the chapter on examining the forearm the same count is given as one item in a long list of skin and pulse signs, with the count intact and without the treatable case. What repeats is the number and what does not repeat is the exception, which is the half that carries the examinability."@en,
        "The two places were read in two different editions, and the reason is a defect of the digitisation and not of the work. In the Sibu congkan text used here, the eleventh juan breaks off inside the chapter on examining the forearm and a block of commentary belonging to a Suwen chapter stands in its place; the passage is absent there. It was read instead in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text of the Lingshu, and both citations say which."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ChinToChestInTheChiefSeats a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das an die Brust gedrückte Kinn in den beiden bevorzugten Sitzhaltungen"@de,
        "The chin pressed to the chest in the two seats the manual prefers"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.37 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.46 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā I.48 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)" ;
    skos:definition "In the two seats the manual singles out as chief, the chin is set firmly against the chest and the gaze is fixed between the brows or at the tip of the nose."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the counterweight to any claim that the manual requires an erect spine throughout. The seats it recommends most warmly close the neck rather than extend it, and the same posture chapter has the tongue set against the roots of the upper teeth while the wind is drawn upward. Uprightness of the trunk is attested; uprightness carried through the neck and head is contradicted in the same work. See prima-materia#29."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hathayoga .

pmc:ChuiqiJinshe a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Qi gegen Schlangen blasen, nah und aus der Ferne"@de,
        "Blowing the qi at snakes, near and at a distance"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 17 (Dengshe), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 17" ;
    skos:definition "Before entering, the practitioner learns the interdiction at home and holds in mind the sun and moon and the four directional animals to guard his body. In the undergrowth he takes three breaths on the left, holds them, and blows them over the vegetation, intending the qi to be red and to fill several tens of li like cloud and mist; those with him, however many, are drawn up in a line and blown over likewise, and thereafter he treads on a snake and it does not stir, and mostly meets none. Meeting one, he takes three breaths on the left facing the sun, holds them, props the tongue against the palate, pinches the gate with the hand, shuts the gate of heaven and stops the door of earth, presses down the snake's head with something and winds it about with the hand, drawing a prison on the ground to hold it; and it will not bite, and until he releases the interdiction by blowing out the qi it cannot leave the prison. Where another has been bitten, he takes three breaths on the left and blows, and the man is healed and the pain stops; and where the man is ten and more li away he may make the qi from afar, calling the man's surname and name, invoking over his own left hand for a man and his own right for a woman, and that man too is healed."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause repeats the rule of the eighth chapter word for word in its operative part: the practitioner blows and incants over his own hand, left for a male patient and right for a female, at a distance, on someone he does not see. It is therefore not a single formula of one chapter but a rule the work states twice, in two different operations, and it is what a search for the palms as an organ of transmission finds instead."@en,
        "The sending organ in both places is the breath, and the hand is where the breath is directed. The chapter has the qi do the work throughout — over the grass, over the snake, over the wound, over the distance — and gives the hand no office except in the distant case, where it is the thing blown upon."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:ChukcheeColdWaterEndsInspiration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kaltes Wasser auf den Kopf beendet die Eingebung"@de,
        "Cold water on the head ends the inspiration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Waldemar Bogoras, The Chukchee, Memoir of the American Museum of Natural History vol. VII (Leiden: E. J. Brill, 1904-1909), p. 433" ;
    skos:definition "The stated means of breaking off the state: the application of cold water to the head of a practitioner in the very heat of his performance would suddenly bring his inspiration to an end, and at ceremonials his neighbours may sprinkle him for that purpose when he practises too long or seems excited to a dangerous pitch."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is a failing condition of the rarest kind: one that a bystander can bring about deliberately, in the middle of the act, with a stated and immediate effect. Most conditions in this holding are set before the act and can only be observed afterwards."@en,
        "The recorder marks his own distance at this one place: he was told this, where the surrounding paragraphs report what he saw. The node keeps the difference rather than levelling it into the rest of the account."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChukcheeShamanism .

pmc:CinwadPuhl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Činwad-Brücke"@de,
        "The Chinvat bridge"@en ;
    dcterms:source "The Book of Arda Viraf, ed. and trans. Martin Haug, E. W. West and Hoshang Jamaspji Asa (Bombay and London: Trübner, 1872), 4.7 and 5.1" ;
    skos:altLabel "Chinvat"@en,
        "Cinvat"@en,
        "Cinwad puhl"@en ;
    skos:definition "The bridge at which the accounting falls due. In the account of the journey under mang it is called very wide and strong, and its width for the traveller who has passed becomes nine javelin-lengths."@en ;
    skos:note "The measure is stated in a unit and is therefore comparable, which most of what the transmissions in this graph say about such passages is not. What the chapters read do not give is the counter-measure, the width for the one who fails; that stands in another book and was not opened here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:CizhenPresupposesJingshen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Nadelung setzt voraus, dass der Geist da ist"@de,
        "Needling presupposes that the spirit is present"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Two passages state it and both state a consequence rather than a degree. Where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured, and the body worn out and the blood exhausted the treatment establishes nothing. Where the five, essence, spirit, hun, po and the intent, are damaged, the needle cannot treat him at all."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Jingshen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:CizhongIntervalDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Wie lange nach dem Treffer ins Depot der Tod eintreten soll"@de,
        "How long after a depot is struck death is said to follow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 16 (Zhen yao jing zhong lun), with the Xin jiao zheng collation notes, in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14",
        "Huangdi neijing suwen 64 (Si shi ci ni cong lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 18",
        "Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing, juan 5 (Zhen jiu jin ji), in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" ;
    skos:definition "Four places in the same canon give differing intervals for the same strike, and a further witness gives another set. The needling-prohibition chapter has the kidney at six days, the lung at three and the spleen at ten. The chapter on the essentials of examination has the heart dying within one circuit rather than in one day, the spleen at five days, the kidney at seven, the lung at five, and adds a strike through the diaphragm after which the patient dies within the year however well he seems to recover. The chapter on needling with and against the four seasons repeats the whole list a third time, agreeing with the needling-prohibition chapter at heart, liver, lung and spleen, giving the kidney's movement sign as sneezing and yawning where the other has sneezing alone, omitting the gall bladder, and adding a summary clause the others do not have: that whoever in needling injures a man's five depots kills him for certain, and that his death is to be known from the movement sign of the depot concerned. The Zhenjiu jiayi jing has the kidney at three days, the spleen at fifteen, and gives the liver's sign as a different character from the one the received Suwen prints. The Song editors record in their collation notes that the older recension of Quan Yuanqi agreed with the Jiayi jing on both numbers, and that Wang Bing changed the liver's sign."@en ;
    skos:note "The commentators do not treat the numbers as observations. The note beside the spleen argues from ten being the completion number of earth, and other commentators have wanted to correct liver and lung to the generation numbers of wood and metal. A number defended from a numerology is not thereby false, but it is not evidence of a counted interval either, and a protocol on this claim would have to begin there."@en,
        "The consequence for this graph is narrower than the dispute. Nothing here says that the intervals are wrong; what it says is that a citation naming one chapter conceals that the same canon carries three answers, and that the earliest reachable witness disagrees with the received text at two of the six places."@en,
        "The dispute is entered because the transmitted text states it about itself and states a verdict on it. The collation note printed with the chapter on the essentials of examination says that these three discourses are all the words of Qi Bo and that their differing is an error of transmission. That is one party's account of the divergence and not a neutral finding, and the graph keeps it as such."@en,
        "The third Suwen chapter was added on 2026-09-10 after being read at the wording, and it changes the balance rather than adding a fourth set of numbers. Two of the three Suwen chapters now agree on five of the six intervals, so the divergence inside the received text is narrower than the earlier state of this node suggested; what remains is the chapter on the essentials of examination against the other two, and the Jiayi jing against all three. The clause that whoever injures the five depots kills for certain stands only in the added chapter, and it is the sentence that turns a list of intervals into a claim that admits no surviving case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:CizhongWuzang ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon .

pmc:ClaviculaNovemDies a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Neun Tage Enthaltung vor dem Kreis, davon die letzten drei ein Fasten"@de,
        "Nine days of continence before the circle, of which the last three are a fast"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter III, with Book II chapters IV and XIII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "Before commencing, master and disciples must abstain with great and thorough continence for nine days from sensual pleasures and from vain and foolish conversation; the second book repeats the term and adds that during the last three days one eats fasting diet once a day, better bread and water only, and on the last day nothing at all. Within the same nine days every instrument must be made, consecrated and exorcised, so that nothing remains to be prepared when the day and hour of the work arrive."@en ;
    skos:note "The dependent step and the prior step are the same practice because the book makes the preparation part of the operation rather than a separate act with its own name. What the node holds is the term: nine days, with three inside them, fixed in advance and countable by anyone present. It is entered at stated necessity and not at prescribed order because the consequence is quoted and not inferred — but the consequence is the general clause, not a penalty peculiar to this step, and that is the whole difficulty this file is about."@en,
        "The term is not the same length in every witness of this corpus. Mathers notes that the paragraph carrying the nine days is omitted in Lansdowne 1202. The Heptameron printed in the Turner collection of 1655 sets nine days and three within them in the same shape, which was read at the wording in that book; the routes between these texts are documented, so the agreement is reception and not a second witness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If thou doest ill in one single point, these experiments or these Arts will not be verified (Book II chapter I); and, of the order of hours and days and of the position of the Moon, without the operation of which thou canst effect nothing (Book I chapter I)."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaScotDisputatio a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob die salomonischen Beschwörungen überhaupt etwas bewirken"@de,
        "Whether the Solomonic conjurations do anything at all"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter I, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889",
        "Reginald Scot, The Discoverie of Witchcraft, Book XV chapter 5, in the reprint of the edition of 1584 edited by Brinsley Nicholson, London: Elliot Stock, 1886, pages 329-330" ;
    skos:definition "The book holds that whoever fears God and prepares the order of hours and days and the position of the moon may easily and thoroughly arrive at the effect and end he desires, and that the spirits, being conjured, come and give what is asked. A work printed in London in 1584 denies it and gives a ground that is neither scriptural nor demonological but a count of the practitioners: he that can be persuaded that these things are true may soon be brought to believe that the moon is made of green cheese; of whom yet was never seen any rich man, or at least that gained any thing that way, or any unlearned man that became learned by that means, or any happy man that could with the help of this art either deliver himself or his friends from adversity, or add unto his estate any point of felicity — yet these men, in all worldly happiness, must needs exceed all others, if such things could be accomplished as it is presupposed."@en ;
    skos:note "The same chapter of 1584 elsewhere reproduces a necromancy of the Solomonic shape — first fast and pray three days and abstain from all filthiness — in order to expose it, and glosses the fasting term of another handbook with the remark that so the cozenage may best be handled. The reading of the term as stagecraft is that author's and is not entered as a node."@en,
        "The second side is worth having because of the form of its argument and not its verdict. It does not dispute that the spirits exist or that the ceremonies are impious; it takes the capacities the books promise — wealth, learning, deliverance — and observes that the people who work the art do not have them. That is an outcome the transmission's own claims predict and it is checkable from outside the session, which is more than the transmission offers anywhere except at the mark on the thief's face."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:ClaviculaOperatioYieldsImperium ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis,
        pmw:ScotDiscoverieOfWitchcraft .

pmc:ClaviculaSociorumPericulum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der Schüler, der nicht gehorcht, leidet und stirbt zuweilen"@de,
        "The disciple who disobeys suffers, and sometimes dies"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book II chapters III and XIII, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "The companions are to be bound by oath to do all that the master orders. Those who shall act otherwise shall suffer and endure many pains and labours, and run into many dangers, which the spirits will cause and procure for them, and for this cause sometimes they shall even die. Conversely, wearing the consecrated pentacles at the breast, they shall be exempt and free from all perils and dangers, provided that they obey the commands of the master and do all that he ordains them. Within the circle they are on no cause whatsoever to move from their assigned places."@en ;
    skos:note "The protection and the penalty share one condition and it is obedience to a person present. That makes the master, not the procedure, the party whose account decides afterwards whether a harm that occurred was a breach or a visitation — and a harm is the only outcome of the two that leaves evidence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ClaviculaOperatioCirculi ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClaviculaThesauriPericulum a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Die Erdgeister warnen den Schatzgräber und töten ihn, wenn er weitermacht"@de,
        "The earth-spirits warn the treasure-digger, and kill him if he persists"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Clavicula Salomonis, Book I chapter XIV, in the English of S. L. MacGregor Mathers, The Key of Solomon the King, London: George Redway, 1889" ;
    skos:definition "Men who undertake a search for hidden treasures are molested and sometimes put to death by the spirits called gnomes. This is not done through avarice, a spirit being incapable of possessing anything; the spirits are enemies of the passions and foresee the evil ends for which the treasure will be used. When they slightly disturb the workers it is a warning to cease from the work, and if the greedy importunity of the workers obliges them to continue notwithstanding the warnings, the spirits, irritated by their despising the same, frequently put the workmen to death."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution that supplies its own reading of the evidence in both directions. A disturbance during the digging is a warning; a death is the penalty for having ignored one. Nothing that can happen at the site is left over to count against the account, and the men who would have to report the warnings are the men the workers are told not to be disturbed by."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ClaviculaCeteraeVirtutes ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ClaviculaSalomonis ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SolomonicGrimoire .

pmc:ClearLightIsAtTheSleepThreshold a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das klare Licht liegt an der Schlafschwelle"@de,
        "The clear light is placed at the sleep threshold"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the clear light of sleep" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:sleepThreshold ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:ClearLightOfSleep ;
    pm:situationGround "The treatise places the luminosity at the moment of falling asleep and trains the practitioner to hold it rather than pass through it unconscious. The file's own note already separates this from a body whose motor system has shut down while the mind stays awake; both fall on this coordinate, and the difference between them belongs in the nodes, not in the axis."@en .

pmc:CoCendNomaide a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bis zum Ende einer nómad, oder zweier, oder dreier"@de,
        "To the end of a nómad, or of two, or of three"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Sanas Cormaic s.v. Imbas forosnai, Irish text in Stokes, Three Irish Glossaries (London 1862), p. 25, English in Cormac's Glossary, trans. O'Donovan (Calcutta 1868), p. 94" ;
    skos:definition "The term the entry puts on the sleep. The poet is watched until everything he is engaged upon is revealed to him, to the end of a nómad or of two or of three, or as long as he was reckoned to be at the offering."@en ;
    skos:note "A counted term with a second clause that uncounts it. The first half gives a unit and three lengths in it; the second half replaces the count with a quantity fixed by something else entirely, the length of time he was supposed to be at the offering. A practitioner whose revelation has not come at the end of the third unit is therefore not out of time, because the alternative clause is still running, and this holding has the same construction from other transmissions under the generalisation that the counted threshold never falls due."@en,
        "The unit is not established here, and the disagreement between the two obtainable translations is recorded at the tradition instance rather than resolved. What is established is that the transmission counts something, that the count runs to three, and that it supplies a second measure that makes the count non-binding."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:InsularCeltic .

pmc:ColdAsSourceProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob Kälte Quelle oder Widerstand ist"@de,
        "Whether cold is a source or a resistance"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of whether exposure to cold supplies the practitioner with something, in the physical sense the word energy carries, or draws on him."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reading, that the claim reports an experience of being charged, is not established here and the correction matters. This node first cited an intervention study as showing that cold exposure raises circulating catecholamines. Read at the wording, that study attributes the rise to the breathing technique — cyclic hyperventilation with breath retention, producing intermittent respiratory alkalosis and hypoxia — and its participants practised breathing, not cold immersion, during the measurement. Cold was one component of a ten-day training and is not the variable the epinephrine measurements isolate. The claim's experiential reading therefore has no evidence in this file, and the study is retained only for what it does show."@en,
        "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, and everything below is written in the voice of a finding. It is not one. The notes record what the evidence appeared to show before any search for opposing work was made, and they are kept in that voice because rewriting them would destroy the reasoning that has to be checked. Read them as the expected result of an examination nobody has finished, not as its outcome. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The claim has two readings and they come out in opposite directions, so the protocol keeps them apart as prima-materia#341 requires. Read physically, it is turned around: cold exposure raises energy expenditure rather than supplying it. The heat produced in the brown adipose tissue that the cold recruits comes from oxidising the body's own substrate, and the deposit is depleted by the work, not filled by it. Cold costs; it does not deliver."@en,
        "The source transmission stands on the other side, and the issue that opened this said so before any evidence was consulted. The tibetan inner-heat literature sets cold as the resistance against which the heat is raised, never as what the heat is raised from. Where the draft and its transmission disagree, the measurements agree with the transmission. That is the reverse of the expected result and is the reason this node is worth having."@en ;
    pm:baseRate "Energy expenditure and substrate use at thermoneutrality in the same participants."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried. No search was made for work disputing the thermogenesis account or failing to replicate it. The reversal recorded in the notes is therefore one side of a question nobody has asked here."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "A. M. Cypess and others, Identification and importance of brown adipose tissue in adult humans, New England Journal of Medicine 360 (2009), no. 15, pp. 1509-1517, doi:10.1056/NEJMoa0810780",
        "M. Kox and others, Voluntary activation of the sympathetic nervous system and attenuation of the innate immune response in humans, Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 111 (2014), no. 20, pp. 7379-7384, doi:10.1073/pnas.1322174111",
        "V. Ouellet and others, Brown adipose tissue oxidative metabolism contributes to energy expenditure during acute cold exposure in humans, Journal of Clinical Investigation 122 (2012), no. 2, pp. 545-552" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The deviation is read off the thermogenesis literature; the second reading off the intervention study cited below" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Practitioners exposed to cold are found to expend less energy, or to end the exposure with greater substrate reserves, than matched participants at thermoneutrality."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:EnergyTakenFromCold .

pmc:CommunicationMustBeInterpretedByAShaman a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Mitteilung setzt einen Deuter voraus"@de,
        "The communication presupposes an interpreter"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), pp. 68-69" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says that the seeker ought to consult a shaman even where the communication is apparently intelligible and easily understood, and that a candidate for the Sun dance must consult his mentor and be guided by him, but it names no consequence for the man who interprets his own. What it does name is one possible content of the interpretation, and that is entered in the definition because it is a stop rule."@en,
        "The stop rule: the communication may prohibit the candidate from dancing the Sun dance, and if it does he is to proceed no further in the matter. A ceremony that can be called off by the very thing it is undertaken to obtain is unusual enough in this graph to be recorded where it stands."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Wowihanble ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ShamanicInterpretation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:CompletionPresupposesGeneration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Vollendungsstufe setzt die Erzeugungsstufe voraus"@de,
        "The completion stage presupposes the generation stage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The order is set by the arrangement of the treatise, which takes the generation-stage meditations before the completion-stage yogas. That the six yogas belong to the completion stage places tummo after the generation stage as well."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:CompletionStage ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:GenerationStage ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:ConcealmentFromTheRecorder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Praxis wird dem Fragenden verborgen"@de,
        "The practice is concealed from the questioner"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hernando Ruiz de Alarcón, Tratado de las supersticiones y costumbres gentílicas que hoy viven entre los indios naturales de esta Nueva España (1629), tratado primero, capítulos I and VI, read in a digital reproduction of the Spanish text in its old orthography which does not name the edition it reproduces" ;
    skos:definition "The recorder states that the people of this land, like those of Peru, hide the matter with the greatest diligence; that they deny it stubbornly even when accused and convicted, until they die impenitent; and that neither the fear of God nor an oath nor anything else avails to make them confess."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison with Peru is the recorder's and is not evidence of any connection between the two. It is entered because it shows he was generalising across two colonial situations, which is one of the ways a report acquires content its informants never supplied."@en,
        "This is the most valuable sentence in what was read, and it cuts two ways. It records a gate: the transmission was held closed against an outsider who had the power to punish, which is a fact about the tradition. And it records the limit of the whole manual as a witness, in the words of the man who wrote it: what he has is what could not be hidden from him. Every other node in this file is to be read against it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NahuaRitual .

pmc:ConcentrationPresupposesVirtue a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Sammlung setzt Sittlichkeit voraus"@de,
        "Concentration presupposes virtue"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.1 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)",
        "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.7-I.8 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    skos:note "The handbook is built as a commentary on a canonical verse in which the one established in virtue develops mind and understanding, and it glosses the order accordingly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Sila ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:ConfessionYieldsAverting a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die Beichte wendet den Zorn ab"@de,
        "The confession turns aside the anger"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Pablo Joseph de Arriaga, Extirpación de la idolatría del Pirú (Lima: Gerónymo de Contreras, 1621), cap. V" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ConfessionBeforeTheIchuri ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Andean ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:AvertingTheAngerOfTheHuacas .

pmc:ConsciousnessCalibrationProtocol a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Ob sich eine Bewusstseinsstufe am Muskeltest ablesen lässt"@de,
        "Whether a level of consciousness can be read off a muscle test"@en ;
    skos:definition "An examination of the claim that the position of a mental or emotional state on a numbered scale can be established by testing the strength of a subject's muscle, as the scale's author reports having done over some quarter of a million times."@en ;
    skos:note "A second reason for caution about the direction of the finding. The source itself names conditions under which its procedure is said to fail: synthetic clothing on the subject, metal jewellery, the voice of the tester. A procedure whose stated interferences are all channels through which an expectation could travel is one whose null result would be expected on ordinary grounds, and whose positive result would need to exclude them. That observation belongs in the protocol and not in a verdict."@en,
        "The counter-search is owed and has not been carried out. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists, some of it from within the field, and a rejection that has read only one side is by SPEC §13 a one-sided dispute and not a refutation. Until it is read, the state of this protocol stays where it is."@en,
        "What the available evidence covers. Three controlled studies bear on the muscle test as a diagnostic instrument, all with blinding, and none of them supports it. A test-retest study on wasp-venom allergy with four examiners, seven patients and twenty bottles each reports an overall kappa of 0.03 with sensitivity around forty per cent and specificity around sixty; a dental double-blind study on materials testing reports the test unreliable; a double-blind randomised study on therapy localisation reports the same. The first states plainly that as a diagnostic tool the method is no more useful than guessing."@en,
        "What the available evidence does not cover, and this is the point. Every one of those studies asks whether the muscle test can identify a substance or a bodily site. None asks whether it can place a mental state on a numbered scale. The evidence therefore strikes the presupposition, that the test yields answers better than chance, and not the claim itself. No blinded replication of the calibrations is known, and the claim is unexamined in the strict sense even though its foundation is not."@en,
        "Why this claim and not another. Almost every claim in this graph is about an inner state for which no procedure exists, and pm:noProcedureDevised is accordingly the commonest state in the holding. This one names its own procedure, the procedure requires no instrument, and blinding it is cheap. Among the claims the graph carries it is the most nearly testable, and that is the reason it was opened."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not carried, and the debt is stated in the notes above. Work claiming reliability for manual muscle testing exists and has not been read; until it is, the negative reading rests on one side."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "H.-J. Staehle, M. J. Koch and T. Pioch, Double-blind Study on Materials Testing with Applied Kinesiology, Journal of Dental Research 84 (2005), no. 11, doi:10.1177/154405910508401119",
        "R. Lüdtke, B. Kunz, N. Seeber and J. Ring, Test-retest-reliability and validity of the Kinesiology muscle test, Complementary Therapies in Medicine 9 (2001), pp. 141-145",
        "S. A. Schwartz and others, A Double-Blind, Randomized Study to Assess the Validity of Applied Kinesiology as a Diagnostic Tool and as a Nonlocal Proximity Effect, Explore 10 (2014), no. 2" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Not devised here. The procedure is the one the source prescribes; the falsifying condition is the ordinary requirement of inter-rater and test-retest agreement, and nobody has yet applied it to the calibrations themselves" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "Blinded testers, given the same states without knowing which, do not agree on their placement above chance; or the same tester, blinded to his earlier results, does not reproduce his own placements."@en ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:HawkinsConsciousnessScale .

pmc:ConsultationPresupposesQualifyingWood a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung setzt sonnenläufiges Holz voraus"@de,
        "Consulting the drum presupposes wood that turns with the sun"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, Of the magicall Ceremonies of the Laplanders, pp. 47-48" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drum must be of such wood, and does not say what follows from using a drum that is not. The strength is the strength the text gives, not the strength the requirement sounds like."@en,
        "This is the condition the graph nearly lost. It was read at the wording in the same run as the rest of this file but reached only a local working copy, not the branch, and was recovered only because an uncommitted file was noticed. Recorded as a process finding in prima-materia#351."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RunicDrumConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:DrumWoodTurningWithTheSun ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:ConsultationPresupposesTaboos a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Befragung setzt die Tabus voraus"@de,
        "The consultation presupposes the taboos"@en ;
    dcterms:source "E. E. Evans-Pritchard, Witchcraft, Oracles and Magic among the Azande, abridged by Eva Gillies (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1976), p. 132" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the source does not merely prescribe the abstinence but says what its want does to the instrument. An unclean man may still address the oracle from a distance, which is a graded rather than an absolute exclusion and is recorded here rather than smoothed away."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Contact of an unclean person with the oracle is certain to destroy its potency, and even the close proximity of an unclean person may have that result."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:BengeConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BengeTaboos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:AzandeOracle .

pmc:ConvergingDescentAcquires a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Was die Seele trägt, wurde beim Abstieg aufgenommen"@de,
        "What the soul carries was taken on during the descent"@en ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the hermetic account of the descent, in which each of the seven rulers gives Man a share of his own ordering, and the ophite account in which the ascending soul must give back or show what belongs to each ruler's realm."@en ;
    skos:note "Recorded because the asymmetry is the finding. A reader who meets the ophite speeches alone cannot tell whether the symbols are acquired, innate or conferred by a rite, and the received accounts settle that question with a doctrine borrowed from the other side of this node."@en,
        "This is the assumption that makes the seven-station ascent intelligible at all, and only one of the two witnesses states it. The hermetic treatise says outright that the rulers gave shares and that Man became a sharer in their nature; the ophite speeches presuppose that something is owed or shown at each gate and never say where it came from. The correspondence is therefore asymmetrical in evidence, not only in wording."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:HermeticDescensusPerHarmoniam,
        pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Same milieu as above: Graeco-Egyptian, second to third century. No priority is asserted between the two accounts, and the dating of the Poimandres is itself disputed." .

pmc:ConvergingGatedAscentInOneWitness a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Dieselbe Sieben-Tor-Architektur, zweimal in einem Buch, und nur einmal mit Planeten"@de,
        "The same seven-gate architecture, twice in one book, and only once with planets"@en ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the Mithraic ladder of metal gates and the ophite gate speeches: both divide the ascent of the soul into gates passed one at a time in a fixed order, and both are preserved in the sixth book of one refutation, nine chapters apart. Only the Mithraic account assigns a planet to each gate; only the ophite account gives the words spoken there."@en ;
    skos:note "The order is what a transfer would have to explain. Lead-Saturn, tin-Venus, bronze-Jupiter, iron-Mercury, alloy-Mars, silver-Moon, gold-Sun is not the sequence in which the ophite speaker meets his gates, and it is not the received order by distance either. A borrowing that reversed or reshuffled the order while keeping the pairing is a larger claim than a borrowing, and nobody has made it."@en,
        "There is no independence here of any kind. One author set the two side by side to make a polemical point, and a second author preserved both while calling the pairing tendentious. The whole correspondence therefore lies inside a single channel, and it counts as one occurrence for any purpose that counts witnesses."@en,
        "This node exists to keep the two apart, not to join them. The architecture is shared and the halves are complementary in a way that invites exactly the merger this graph refuses: one account has the planets and no speeches, the other the speeches and no planets, and a table that seats each ophite ruler on a planet is the merger, performed silently. See prima-materia#595."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:MithraicKlimaxHeptapylos,
        pmc:MithraicPylaiPlanetarum,
        pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Both accounts stand in Origen, Contra Celsum VI, at chapters 22 and 31, and both are quoted from the same lost work of Celsus, who adduces the Mithraic ladder expressly so that the Christian rites may be examined alongside it. No transmission between the two cults is claimed; what is documented is that the pairing itself is the work of the reporting author." .

pmc:ConvergingGradedAscent a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Seele durchläuft sieben Stationen einzeln, und an jeder wird etwas verhandelt"@de,
        "The soul passes seven stations one at a time, and something is transacted at each"@en ;
    skos:definition "The correspondence between the ophite gate speeches and the hermetic surrender of energies: in both, the ascent after death is divided into seven stations that are passed singly and in order, and at each one a particular thing is transacted rather than the passage being granted as a whole."@en ;
    skos:note "A second difference decides whether either can be examined. The hermetic passage names the seven things; the ophite speeches name the gates and never the passions. Every table that pairs an ophite archon with a vice is therefore doing what this node refuses to do: filling one witness from the other."@en,
        "The correspondence is asserted by the compiler and by neither source. What is shared is the form — seven, singly, in order, with a transaction at each — and it is worth naming because it is not the trivial part. A doctrine of ascent does not have to be graded: it could be one passage, or a judgement, or a threshold. These two divide it the same way."@en,
        "What is not shared is what is transacted, and the difference runs in opposite directions. The ophite passer brings something and names it: a symbol of life, his own beard, the spirit of a virgin, the symbol of the mother. The hermetic one leaves something and it is named for him: growth and waning, the device of evils, the guile of the desires, arrogance, daring, the striving for wealth, ensnaring falsehood. One ascends by asserting what he has, the other by being emptied of what he was given. Reading the two as one doctrine of shedding garments takes the hermetic half and supplies it to the other."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:HermeticRedditioEnergiarum,
        pmc:OphiteSymbolumOstensum,
        pmc:OphiteTransitusLiber ;
    pm:transmissionPath "Both corpora are Graeco-Egyptian of the second and third centuries and circulate in the same milieu; the hermetic writings and the diagram Origen obtained are separated by neither language, region nor period, and the ophite material carries names Origen himself derives partly from magic and partly from the Hebrew scriptures, which is the same pool the hermetic and papyrological material draws on. No transmission from one text to the other is claimed; what is documented is that no route needs to be found." .

pmc:CountedThresholdNeverFallsDue a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die gezählte Schwelle wird nie fällig"@de,
        "The counted threshold never falls due"@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation is containment and not duplication, and one node is shared. That statement is about operations whose success cannot be established apart from them at all; this one is about the narrower class that also names a number, and it says something the broader one cannot: that the device is supplied at the exact point where the claim had made itself checkable."@en,
        "The counter-search over the holding found two things that come close and neither of which falls inside the scope. The Kongo corpus reports a charm sold as making the purchaser proof against the gun, and records that when it was seen that the purchasers died like ordinary mortals the traffic ceased; that is a failure not explained away, but it is a single trial and no count is named. The Mithraic ascent material, in a hostile report, says what happens if the operation does not work and the answer is that the operator dies; again no count. Both are entered in the holding and neither is a counted threshold, which is why the scope clause of the statement above says what it says."@en,
        "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. Two of the three claims come from one transmission and the third from another with which a route is not in dispute, both Chinese and both within a few centuries. What the three establish is therefore a property of that stream. The way to widen it is not to restate it more boldly but to look for counted thresholds elsewhere: the counted repetitions of the Notory Art are in this holding and attach no rule of either kind to a completed count, which is a silence and not a third case."@en,
        "The three claims do the same work by two different devices. The recitation of the five thousand words is to fetch the cloud chariot at ten thousand readings, and the same sentence says that a reader who has completed the ten thousand and not departed is to read it twice a month until it comes. The elixir is finished when the gold forms, and where the gold does not form the batch is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, whereupon, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form. The third works the other way: three brothers recited the same book, one reached ten thousand and rose, and the other two are reported at nine thousand seven hundred and thirty-three, the shortfall caused by their having set the book down to look at a deer and made irrecoverable by the book's destruction. The exact number is stated so that the failure has a place to attach to."@en,
        "What would refute it, and it is an ordinary thing to look for: a transmission that names a count, and says that a practitioner who completes the count without the effect has established something about the operation — that it does not work, that it is not for him, that the recipe is to be revised — rather than something about himself. The statement is about what the transmission provides, not about what practitioners did, so a single passage of that kind settles it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:JinchengYaocheng,
        pmc:ShangqingWanbianYunjia,
        pmc:ShangqingZhoushiSanxiong ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission fixes a number of repetitions at which a prescribed operation is to take effect, and the effect is not establishable apart from the operation itself, the same transmission provides in advance for the number being reached without the effect: either by prescribing that the operation be continued past the number, or by assigning the shortfall to a nameable act of the operator. It does not leave the completed count without effect standing as an outcome that would tell against the claim."@en .

pmc:CrookedLimbsIfTheOfficeIsRefused a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das verweigerte Amt wird am Leib bezahlt"@de,
        "The office refused is paid for in the body"@en ;
    dcterms:source "S. M. Shirokogoroff, Psychomental Complex of the Tungus (London: Kegan Paul, Trench, Trubner & Co., 1935), chap. XXVIII, sect. 128, p. 349" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity, and the consequence is quoted rather than paraphrased because it is the sharpest one in the file. The case is a single one, among the Khingan Tungus: a girl of fifteen entered by a clan spirit, taught by an old shaman to distinguish the questions asked by the assistants, a shaman at the time of the report for some twenty-five years."@en,
        "One case is a case and not a rate. What makes it worth a node is that the transmission states a specific bodily outcome for the refusal, which is the shape a condition of failure has, and not a general misfortune."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "If she had not become a shaman, her arms or legs or neck, or back would have been crooked for life."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:TungusCandidateCondition ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:TungusMasteringOfSpirits ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SiberianShamanism .

pmc:Daena a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Daēnā"@de,
        "Daena"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hadhoxt Nask, fragment 2.7-11, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 23 (Oxford: Clarendon, 1883)" ;
    skos:altLabel "daena"@en,
        "den"@en ;
    skos:definition "What meets the soul at the end of the third night after death, in the shape of a woman. The oldest witness read here fixes three things a later reader can hold the transmission to: the moment, the end of the third night, after a wind that comes sweet-scented from the south; the appearance, of the size of a maid in her fifteenth year, fair, bright, white-armed, strong, tall-formed, high-standing, thick-breasted, beautiful of body, noble, of a glorious seed; and the answer she gives when asked who she is, that she is the soul's own conscience."@en ;
    skos:note "The account of the journey under mang describes the same encounter and gives the figure no age at all. The fifteenth year stands in the older Avestan fragment and not in the Pahlavi book, and the two are kept apart here rather than merged, because a reader who takes the age from one and the setting from the other is reading a text no witness gives."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:DaenaCounterpart a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Gegenstück, das die Seele des Bösen trifft"@de,
        "The counterpart met by the soul of the wicked"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Hadhoxt Nask, fragments 2.15 and 3.25-33, trans. James Darmesteter, Sacred Books of the East 23 (Oxford: Clarendon, 1883)" ;
    skos:definition "The same structure with every term reversed: at the end of the third night a foul-scented wind from the north, and four steps that lead to the hell of evil thought, of evil word, of evil deed, and to endless darkness, against the four that lead to the paradise of good thought, good word, good deed, and endless lights."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the symmetry is the point. A transmission that gives both outcomes in the same form, with the same number of steps and the same sequence, is making a claim with two branches rather than a promise; the passages read state the second as fully as the first."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Zoroastrian .

pmc:DancingPresupposesOfferingAndFlowingBlood a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Tanz setzt eine Gabe und eine blutende Wunde voraus"@de,
        "The dance presupposes an offering and a wound that bleeds"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. R. Walker, The Sun Dance and Other Ceremonies of the Oglala Division of the Teton Dakota (Anthropological Papers AMNH, vol. XVI, part II, New York, 1917), p. 61 and p. 93" ;
    skos:note "Entered at stated necessity: the corpus does not merely order the two before the dance but says what their absence costs. The wound may be as small as the skin of a louse in the first form and as large as the dancer wills."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:fieldAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Before beginning to dance the candidate must make an acceptable offering to the Sun and have a wound that will cause his blood to flow while he dances; when the blood flows as a token it is the surest guarantee of sincerity, and without such a guarantee the people or the Sun may doubt the professed purposes of the dancer."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:WiwanyangWacipi ;
    pm:fieldRecord "Spoken by named Oglala shamans, chiefly Sword, Tyon, Ringing-shield, Bad-wound and No-flesh, and by Finger on 25 March 1914; recorded by the agency physician J. R. Walker at Pine Ridge over his years of service there, closed December 1916; given in Lakota, part of it written out in Lakota by Sword and Tyon and translated by Burt Means, Thomas Mills, Bruce Means and Antoin Herman; the paper prints the translations only, so the Lakota wording is not carried." ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:OfferingAndWoundBeforeTheDance ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:LakotaRitual .

pmc:DanshaHuanfan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Zinnober wird zu Quecksilber und kehrt zurück"@de,
        "Cinnabar goes to mercury and comes back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Ge Hong, Baopuzi neipian 4 (Jindan), in the Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou reprint, DZ 1185, juan 4" ;
    skos:definition "The observation on which the chapter rests its case for minerals over plants. Grasses and trees, burnt, are ash at once; cinnabar, burnt, becomes mercury, and by accumulated transformation it becomes cinnabar again. Therefore, the chapter argues, it is far removed from common plants and can make a man long-lived. It adds that the common people do not know that mercury comes out of cinnabar, and that told so they will not believe it, objecting that cinnabar is a red thing and asking whence it should become this white thing, and that cinnabar is a stone, and that burnt stones all become ash."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter also reports the objection it met, and the objection is the same kind of argument as its own: cinnabar is a stone, and stones burn to ash. Both sides reason from what a substance does in the fire to what it will do in a man. The one that happened to have looked was right about the fire."@en,
        "The observation is correct and repeatable, which is rare in this collection and is what makes the passage worth a node of its own. What it does not carry is the step: a substance that returns to its own form after transformation is not thereby a substance that keeps a body from returning to earth. The chapter states the observation and the conclusion and leaves the step between them unstated."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Waidan .

pmc:DescentPresupposesDepth a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Abstieg setzt die Tiefe voraus, die den Atem sammelt"@de,
        "The descent presupposes the depth that gathers the breath"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125" ;
    skos:note "Recorded at stated necessity because a consequence is quotable, which is unusual: most sequences in this collection state an order and leave the consequence unsaid. The strength rests on the closing line, not on the chain of conditionals, which on its own would carry only a prescribed order."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The inscription closes by setting the two outcomes against each other: to go with the sequence is to live, to go against it is to die. The consequence is stated for the order as a whole, and every stage is joined to the one before it by the same conditional form." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:XingqiDescent ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:XingqiDepth ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Daoist .

pmc:DieshiYieldsZhilai a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Abzählen bringt das Wissen des Kommenden hervor"@de,
        "The counting yields the knowledge of what is coming"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhouyi, Xici A, the section beginning with the number of the great expansion and the section on prognostication, in the Wang Bi and Han Kangbo recension; read in the digital text of the Kanseki Repository, which names no printing" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:Dieshi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseDivination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:JishuZhilai .

pmc:DirectKnowledgesPresupposeConcentration a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die höheren Kenntnisse setzen die Sammlung voraus"@de,
        "The direct knowledges presuppose concentration"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga I.11 (trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The handbook states that the six kinds of direct knowledge are arrived at with the support of perfected concentration and by nothing besides that." ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Cetopariyanana,
        pmc:Dibbacakkhu,
        pmc:Dibbasota,
        pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada .

pmc:DirectionIsNotThePhaseOfBreathing a pm:Converging ;
    rdfs:label "Die Richtung trägt eine gestufte Bahn, nicht die Atemphase"@de,
        "Direction is carried by a graded course, not by the phase of breathing"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Svātmārāma, Haṭhayogapradīpikā II.47 (Sanskrit vulgate; Sinh 1915)",
        "Xingqi yupei ming, in D. Harper, Early Chinese Medical Literature (1998), p. 125",
        "Zhangjiashan Yinshu, slips 36 to 37, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)" ;
    skos:definition "Two traditions assign direction inside the body to something other than inhaling and exhaling. One assigns it to two winds driven against each other in a single operation; the other to a place in a graded sequence, with descent in the middle of it and ascent at its end. Neither pairs a direction with a phase of the breath."@en ;
    skos:note "A second correspondence sits alongside this one and is deliberately not asserted as a separate node yet: both traditions sanction the wrong handling of the breath with death, the one saying that the breath kills the practitioner who forces it, the other that going against the sequence is to die. Two death sanctions are a thinner finding than they look, since a warning of death is the commonest form a strong prohibition takes anywhere, and the two do not agree on what the fault is: one names haste and force, the other names reversal of order. Recorded here so that the next reader can weigh it rather than rediscover it."@en,
        "A third member was added on 2026-09-16 and it changes the asymmetry the note above reports. The Pulling Book from tomb 247 at Zhangjiashan states a direction taken inside the body and the outcome of its taking it: with a staff in one hand, the face to a wall and the breath held, one foot treading the wall, when the qi of the head flows downward the foot will not be numb, the head will not swell and the nose will not be blocked (pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing). The breath is held throughout, so the direction is not carried by a phase of it; what is varied is the posture and the side. The agreement is on the denial and not on what takes the denial's place — the manual has two winds driven against each other, the jade inscription a graded course, and this one a posture — and the node is worded for the denial, so the third member belongs to it."@en,
        "The independence is asymmetric and the node says so rather than claiming more than it has. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era on the reading this collection follows, which is earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China; for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions, so its side of the correspondence cannot be declared contact-free with the same confidence. What the two share is therefore better read as one early independent witness and one late witness of uncertain independence, which is weaker than a convergence of two early ones and stronger than a citation chain."@en,
        "What the third member is worth is its date and the way its date is known. The jade inscription is dated on a reading of its script and its content, which is a scholarly judgement and can be revised. This manuscript is dated by the tomb it was sealed in, between 186 and 156 before this era, and nothing was written into it after that. For the independence question that is a different kind of ground: the earlier reasoning had to argue that no route of contact is documented, and this one can say that no route could have reached the object after a fixed year. It does not make the fifteenth-century manual independent, and the asymmetry below still holds; what it does is put two early witnesses on the side that was carrying one."@en ;
    pm:convergingConcept pmc:OpposedDirectionsOfPranaAndApana,
        pmc:XingqiDescent,
        pmc:YinshuQiXiaXing ;
    pm:independenceGround "One side carries the claim and the other has been withdrawn from it. The jade inscription is dated to the fourth or third century before the common era, earlier than any documented transmission of Indian breath technique into China, and for that witness there is no route of contact to be had. The manual is fifteenth-century and stands downstream of centuries of Buddhist traffic in both directions; it cannot be declared contact-free, and it was listed here as an independent attestation until this ground was required, which is what the requirement is for. Neither witness is an ethnographic gathering, so no recorder's feedback is at issue." ;
    pm:independentAttestation pmt:Daoist .

pmc:DistantPerceptionFromAVoluntaryState a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Wahrnehmung des Unerreichbaren aus einem willentlich betretenen Zustand"@de,
        "Perception of what is out of range, from a state entered at will"@en ;
    skos:note "No pm:Testing points at this node, and that is its function rather than a defect. SPEC §16 makes the untested generalizations the queue for the examining layer; this one heads it, because it rests on more attested claims and more widely separated transmissions than anything else the holding can currently form."@en,
        "Seven attested claims from five transmissions: the Sámi journey made while the body lies still, two applications of saṃyama in the Yogasūtra, two of the graded degrees of the Midewiwin, the Iglulik spirit calling, and the Lakota sending of the sacred stones. Each names its own work and passage; this node names none, because none of them says what it says."@en,
        "The derivation is contestable at two points and both are left visible. First, whether the four transmissions describe one kind of practice at all: the Sámi journey, saṃyama, the Midewiwin degrees and the Iglulik calling agree in that the state is entered deliberately and differ in almost everything else. Second, whether reporting on a distant place and reading another mind are one effect or two — the sources treat them as neighbouring and this node treats them as one, which is a decision and not a finding."@en,
        "The seventh claim was added after the sixth and its addition is the only one that changed the shape of the derivation, so it is recorded rather than absorbed. The Lakota case is the first in this set that comes with narrated cases attached: a stone sent to find a rifle dropped into a river at a named place, and a stone sent to bring a buffalo in front of a fur trader who had staked ten articles against it. Cases do not make evidence — nobody devised the procedure and nobody recorded an outcome that could have been checked by a third party — but they are what a protocol would have to address, and no other claim under this node has any. Against that stands the same objection as for the Midewiwin material, since the recording is of nineteen eleven to nineteen fourteen; and beside it the fact that the same transmission names the cause to which a failure of the instrument is attributed, so that its own successes cannot be counted as a run."@en,
        "Why the wording is as heavy as it is. The traditions say that the capacity is reached; a statement that repeated that could not fail at anything. What can fail is a comparison, so the statement is written as one, with the comparison group named — people working from the same prior information without the state. Whoever thinks the sentence overreaches should attack that clause, because it is the clause that carries the whole claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:FourthDegreeYieldsThoughtReading,
        pmc:JourneyYieldsDistantSight,
        pmc:SamyamaYieldsDivyaSrotra,
        pmc:SamyamaYieldsParacittaJnana,
        pmc:SecondDegreeYieldsDistantHearing,
        pmc:SpiritCallingYieldsRecoveryOfLostThings,
        pmc:TunkanYieldsDistantKnowledge ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a practitioner enters a stated inner state at will and then reports on a person, place or object outside the range of ordinary perception, the reports agree with independently established facts more often than reports produced, from the same prior information, by people who have not entered such a state."@en .

pmc:DistinctionDesEsprits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unterscheidung guter und schlechter Geister"@de,
        "Telling a good spirit from a bad one"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Allan Kardec, Le Livre des Médiums, seconde partie, chapitre XXIV, sections 263-264" ;
    skos:altLabel "distinction des bons et des mauvais Esprits"@fr ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the whole system rests on, since every claim in it arrives through a communication that may be false. The criterion given is not evidential but linguistic and moral: spirits are judged as men are judged, by their language, and goodness and benevolence are held to be the essential attributes of purified spirits."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Spiritism .

pmc:DistinctivenessAgainstDissolution a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Die Unterschiedenheit als Streben des Geschöpfs gegen die Auflösung"@de,
        "Distinctiveness as the striving of the creature against dissolution"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1916" ;
    dcterms:source "C. G. Jung, Septem Sermones ad Mortuos, Sermo I (English translation of the 1916 private printing, Gnostic Society Library electronic edition)" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a creature which ceases to distinguish falls into the undifferentiated fullness, ceases to be a creature and is given over to dissolution in nothingness, so that its natural striving runs towards distinctiveness against a primeval and perilous sameness; and that the striving is nevertheless not after difference but after the creature's own being."@en ;
    skos:note "Placed on the telic axis as pm:individuatingTelos. The placement is a reading and rests on the two refusals the passage makes in the same breath: dissolution is called the death of the creature, and the striving is said not to be after difference. Stated here rather than left implicit so that it can be contradicted."@en,
        "Recorded because it is the only position located so far that names both ends of the axis as a loss and then states a third movement. Dissolution into the fullness is called the death of the creature outright, which rules out the unitive end; and the striving is expressly not after difference as it is thought, which rules out the separative one. What remains is a striving after one's own being, and the source gives it the name principium individuationis."@en,
        "The attribution is why this is an origination and not a witness of gnostic transmission. The author printed the text under the name of a second-century teacher of Alexandria who did not write it, and it continues to be read and cited as gnostic teaching. pm:circulatesAs records that, and the node makes no judgement about the author's own reading of the ancient material."@en,
        "The god named above the god of the pairs of opposites belongs to the same argument and not to a separate doctrine: the qualities of the fullness are given as pairs, and what is set above them unites the full and the empty. A reading that takes this for a third party in a pantheon takes the figure for the argument."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A gnostic text of the second century, printed under the name of Basilides of Alexandria and read as ancient teaching rather than as a work written in 1916." ;
    pm:originatedBy "C. G. Jung" ;
    pm:telicOrientation pm:individuatingTelos .

pmc:DivineDreamIsNotOrdinarySleep a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Der göttliche Traum ist nicht der gewöhnliche Schlaf"@de,
        "The divine dream is not ordinary sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Iamblichus, De mysteriis III.2-3 (ed. des Places 1966)" ;
    skos:definition "The warning that the mass of dreams occurring in full sleep carries no divination, and that only the state at the edge of sleep is treated as a sign of divine presence."@en ;
    skos:note "Load-bearing against any claim that sleep as such is an access. The source that comes closest in the whole collection to naming the threshold state is also the one that refuses the wider claim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Theopemptos ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:DiviningPresupposesPupilage a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Befragen setzt die Lehrzeit voraus"@de,
        "Divining presupposes the pupilage"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the training of a candidate for the office of Babalawo" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a property of the passage rather than of the tradition. The source sets the pupilage, its minimum length and the division of its course, and says nothing about what befalls a man who divines without it. A stated consequence would raise this to a necessity; none stands in what was read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:IfaCasting ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:BabalawoPupilage ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:DivyaCaksusIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das göttliche Auge wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt"@de,
        "The divine eye is exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.26" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:DivyaCaksus ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra reads bhuvanajñānaṃ sūrye saṃyamāt: knowledge of the worlds from saṃyama on the sun. The operator is named in the sūtra itself, and its state is fixed at III.1-4."@en .

pmc:DivyaSrotraIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das göttliche Ohr wird in abgezogener Vertiefung geübt"@de,
        "The divine ear is exercised in withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.1-4",
        "Patañjali, Yogasūtra III.41" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:DivyaSrotra ;
    pm:situationGround "The sūtra has the divine ear follow from saṃyama on the relation between the organ of hearing and the ether. The operator is named in the sūtra itself, and its state is fixed at III.1-4."@en .

pmc:DogonSiriusExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Steht die Kenntnis des Begleiters in der Überlieferung?"@de,
        "Is the knowledge of the companion found in the transmission?"@en ;
    skos:definition "A protocol addressing the claim that Dogon transmission carries knowledge of an invisible companion of Sirius. It asks whether the star names and the doctrine can be found among Dogon outside the circle of the original informants, and whether any account of them leads anywhere but back to the recording work."@en ;
    skos:note "Status of what follows. The state above is procedure without cases, set when the counter-search requirement was introduced, because this node does not record whether a search for opposing work was made. Its notes are older than the requirement and are kept unchanged; whoever documents the search restores the case-bearing state. See prima-materia#386."@en,
        "The counter-testimony has not been read at the wording in this run. A reply appeared in the same journal in the following volume, and a further exchange in the two years after; the deviation recorded here therefore rests on one side of a controversy that has two. See prima-materia#252. Under SPEC §13 this state is a one-sided dispute waiting for its second side, not a refutation, and the state below is set on the cases and not on the conclusion."@en,
        "The one trace that remains is itself the finding. Informants in that village say that in former times people knew of a star appearing in the year of the ceremony, and further enquiry leads back to the recorder. What looks like independent survival is feedback from the published account into the field it describes, and any later confirmation must be tested against that path before it counts."@en,
        "The re-study reports the ethnographic facts as straightforward. Dogon know Sirius as a star and call it the hunter's star; knowledge of the stars matters neither in daily life nor in ritual, the sun and the phases of the moon being what reckoning runs on. No Dogon outside the circle of the original informants had heard the star names the account turns on, and no one, not even inside that circle, had heard or understood that Sirius was double. The scheduling of the ceremony is done in several ways in the village the account draws on, none of them astronomical."@en,
        "The re-study's explanation of how the account arose is not adopted here as a finding, only recorded: that an informant spoke of stars of different generations, meaning two adjacent visible stars held as father and son to Sirius as grandfather, and that the recorder, who had read astronomy in Paris, made a double and triple system of it. That is a reconstruction of an intention and cannot be examined; the absence of the doctrine in the field can."@en ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchNotCarried ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Not documented. Predates the requirement; see the note at pmc:TummoHeatExamination."@en ;
    pm:evidenceFrom "Walter E. A. van Beek, Dogon Restudied: A Field Evaluation of the Work of Marcel Griaule, Current Anthropology 32/2 (1991), pp. 149-150 with its footnote 10, and pp. 156-157" ;
    pm:examinationState pm:procedureWithoutCases ;
    pm:examinedBy "Walter E. A. van Beek, field re-study among the Dogon of Sanga and Yugo Doguru, reported 1991" ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A report of the star names or of the doctrine of an invisible companion from Dogon speakers outside the original circle of informants, where the chain of the report can be shown not to run back through the published account; or a fixation of the doctrine in any text or record datable before nineteen thirty-one. Either would return the claim to contested and undo the deviation recorded here." ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-01"^^xsd:date ;
    pm:tests pmc:DogonKnowledgeOfSiriusCompanion .

pmc:DoingPresupposesBeing a pm:Originating ;
    rdfs:label "Es wird nichts getan, solange niemand da ist, der tut"@de,
        "Nothing is done until there is someone to do it"@en ;
    dcterms:date "1949" ;
    dcterms:source "P. D. Ouspensky, In Search of the Miraculous: Fragments of an Unknown Teaching (London 1950), in the first talks on the human machine and in the chapter on the absence of unity" ;
    skos:definition "The claim that a person is a machine governed by outward influences, that everything happens and nobody does anything, and that in order to do it is first necessary to be; together with the account of a person as lacking unity and consisting of a multiplicity of I's rather than one."@en ;
    skos:note "It bears directly on the disagreement recorded in convergences/left-hand-path-divergence.ttl. Both sides there dispute what becomes of the self at the end; this source holds that at the beginning there is no single self to be kept or surrendered, and that one has to be made first. That is not a third answer to their question but a claim that the question has a precondition neither states."@en,
        "The interest for this graph is the order of precedence. The claim is not that people do the wrong thing but that the doer is not yet assembled, which puts every question of method downstream of a question about who is applying it. A path chosen by a multiplicity is chosen by whichever part happens to be present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:circulatesAs "A finding of an older school, transmitted rather than argued." ;
    pm:originatedBy "G. I. Gurdjieff, in the record of P. D. Ouspensky" .

pmc:DouleursCritiquesCommePreuve a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der von der Operation verursachte Schmerz gilt als Beweis ihres Wirkens"@de,
        "The pain the operation causes is counted as proof that it works"@en ;
    dcterms:source "J. P. F. Deleuze, Practical Instruction in Animal Magnetism, trans. Thomas C. Hartshorn, revised ed. (New York: D. Appleton & Co., 1843), chap. I, § 31, p. 27, chap. III, pp. 48-49, and chap. VI, p. 111" ;
    skos:definition "The operation often excites pain in the part where the disease is located and renews old and slumbering pains; these are produced by the efforts nature makes to triumph over the malady, are but transient, and the patient always finds himself better after having experienced them, which is what distinguishes critical pains from those produced by the progress of the disease. The patient is told that these pains prove the action is powerful, that they are necessary to subdue the disease, that he is to bear them without speaking of them to anyone, and that he is not even to ask the operator to calm them."@en ;
    skos:note "The distinguishing criterion offered — that critical pains are transient and followed by improvement — is retrospective, so it cannot be applied at the moment when the patient has to decide. A patient in pain on the tenth day cannot tell from the passage whether he is in the case that requires fortitude or the case that requires renunciation, and the transmission does not supply a test that would tell him."@en,
        "The first of the three clauses that cancel the stopping rules, and the most direct. Under the rule of nearly a month the patient is to renounce the practice if he grows worse; under this one, growing worse is the sign that it is working. The two instructions are eight pages apart in the same chapter sequence and are addressed to the same person."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Mesmerism .

pmc:DreamDivinationPresupposesPurityOfTheVehicle a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumdivination setzt die Reinheit des Fahrzeugs voraus"@de,
        "Dream divination presupposes purity of the vehicle"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Synesius of Cyrene, De insomniis" ;
    skos:note "The condition is on the state of the seer, not on a technique of sleeping. Recorded as order rather than necessity because the treatise makes the clarity of the dream depend on the vehicle without stating at that place what befalls one who proceeds with an unpurified one. Chapter divisions vary between editions; the work and its subject are given so that the passage can be found in whichever edition is at hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:DreamDivination ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:PneumaticVehicle ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:NeoplatonicTheurgy .

pmc:DreamYogaIsPerformedInDreaming a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga wird im Traum ausgeführt"@de,
        "Dream yoga is performed within the dream"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005), the yoga of the dream state" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:dreamingWithin ;
    pm:situatedNode pmp:DreamYoga ;
    pm:situationGround "The exercises are set out as apprehending, multiplying and transforming what appears within the dream, which places the performance inside the state and not at its edge. That the practitioner knows he dreams is what the training is for and is not a separate coordinate."@en .

pmc:DreamYogaPresupposesClearLight a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Traumyoga setzt das Halten des klaren Lichts voraus"@de,
        "Dream yoga presupposes holding the clear light of sleep"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Tsongkhapa, A Book of Three Inspirations (trans. Mullin 2005)" ;
    skos:note "The treatise takes the recognition of the clear light before the training in the dream. It does not state at that place what befalls one who begins elsewhere, which is why the strength is order and not necessity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:DreamYoga ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ClearLightOfSleep ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:TibetanTantric .

pmc:DrumMustBeInherited a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Trommel muss ererbt sein"@de,
        "The drum must be inherited"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Knud Leem, An Account of the Laplanders of Finmark, in John Pinkerton (ed.), A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels, vol. I (London: Longman, Hurst, Rees and Orme, 1808), chap. XXI, p. 474" ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity: the source says the drums were not thought sufficiently fit for their uses unless got by inheritance, and does not say what follows from using one that was not. The rule sits oddly beside the same recorder's report of a practitioner explaining how his own drum was made, and the oddity is left standing rather than resolved."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:RunicDrumConsultation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:SunCourseRule ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:DurationTracksDistance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Dauer des Liegens folgt der Entfernung und endet bei einem Tag"@de,
        "The time he lies still follows the distance, and stops at a day"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johannes Schefferus, The History of Lapland (Oxford: at the Theater, 1674), chap. XI, p. 57" ;
    skos:definition "How long he lies is stated to depend on how near or far the place is of which he makes enquiry — an hour or two for a near one — and to have an upper bound: the time never exceeds twenty-four hours, let the place be at never so great a distance."@en ;
    skos:note "A quantity that varies with a measurable quantity, and a ceiling that does not. Both halves are unusual: the first makes the claim comparable across cases, and the second makes it refutable by a single case that runs longer. Nothing else in this file states a number."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SamiNoaidi .

pmc:Dvadasanta a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Ende der Zwölf"@de,
        "The end of the twelve"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Vijñānabhairava 28 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 50 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)",
        "Vijñānabhairava 51 (Sanskrit; verse numbering of Dyczkowski and the current editions)" ;
    skos:definition "A limit named as the end of twelve, at which the mind or the risen power comes to rest. The verses give the number and leave the unit unstated."@en ;
    skos:note "The count itself fluctuates. What is verse 50 in the numbering used here appears as 51 in other editions, and the following verse shifts with it. A reader working from a different edition should expect the two verses cited here to sit one number further on."@en,
        "Two things this node deliberately does not say. It does not fix the unit: the base text says end-of-twelve, and the reading that makes this twelve finger-breadths from the tip of the nose belongs to the commentarial layer, not to the verse. And it does not fix the place: the exegesis of Lakṣmaṇjoo counts several such limits in the body, at the crown, the brows, the throat, the base of the heart and the navel, and the verse that follows says outright that it may be reached in any manner and in any place. Nothing here yields a fixed radius. See prima-materia#38."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:SaivaSakta .

pmc:EaAndMardukInstructTheExorcist a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Ea unterwies den Beschwörer und legte ihm die Beschwörung in den Mund"@de,
        "Ea instructed the exorcist and put the incantation in his mouth"@en ;
    dcterms:source "KAR 31 (VAT 8803) obv. 1-4 and rev. 26; Udug-hul 8: 25-26 and 30-31 (M. J. Geller, Healing Magic and Evil Demons, BAM 8, De Gruyter 2016, pp. 38-40)" ;
    skos:note "Stated by the exorcist about himself, in the first person, inside the text he recites: I am the messenger of the great gods, Ea and Marduk have instructed me comprehensively, I orally render their pure incantation, I carry out the words they say. The eighth tablet has him say it of himself in the same words and then name the transfer exactly: the great lord Ea has sent me and made his incantation acceptable for my mouth."@en,
        "The ascription is to a god and the attesting mode is textual and not third-party, because what was read is the passage in which the claim stands and not a report that somebody made it. Whether the office was in fact instructed by Ea is not a question this graph can put; that the transmission grounds the office there rather than in a lineage of teachers is."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:AsipuKapirigEridu ;
    pm:ascribedTo pmc:Ea ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:MesopotamianExorcism .

pmc:EighthHogonTeaching a pm:Attributing ;
    rdfs:label "Der achte Häuptling lehrte die Menschen die Merkmale des Sterns"@de,
        "The eighth chief taught men the characteristics of the star"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Marcel Griaule and Germaine Dieterlen, Un système soudanais de Sirius, Journal de la Société des Africanistes 20/2 (Paris: Société des Africanistes, 1950), pp. 282-284" ;
    skos:definition "The account that the eighth supreme chief, having discovered the star, escaped the sacrifice his seven predecessors had undergone by feigning death, returned claiming to have been inside the star and to know its secrets, and then taught men its characteristics and reordered the reckoning of time."@en ;
    skos:note "An ascription inside a myth of origin, and the article gives it as such: it is offered expressly as an indication of the changes and combinations of reckoning that appear in the course of what the recorders put in quotation marks as the history of the Blacks."@en ;
    pm:ascribedCapacity pmc:KnowledgeOfSeedStarCharacteristics ;
    pm:ascribedTo "the eighth supreme chief, in the Dogon account of the origin of the sixty-year reckoning" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Dogon .

pmc:EjiOgbeFirstOfTheSigns a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Eji Ogbe als erstes der Zeichen"@de,
        "Eji Ogbe as the first of the signs"@en ;
    dcterms:source "James Johnson, Yoruba Heathenism, the account of the Odu and their number" ;
    skos:definition "The sign held to be the principal one, called the leader, the commander and the king. Its appearance on the divining bowl is taken to mean that the message delivered is of very great weight."@en ;
    skos:note "A stated order of rank among the signs, and therefore a place where a later reader can check whether a modern list has kept the tradition's own order or imposed another."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:mediatedAttestation ;
    pm:readVia "R. E. Dennett, At the Back of the Black Man's Mind (London: Macmillan, 1906), appendix, extract from Yoruba Heathenism" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Ifa .

pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeartIsWithdrawnAbsorption a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Das Fasten des Herzens liegt bei abgezogener Vertiefung"@de,
        "The fasting of the heart is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Zhuangzi 4, in James Legge (trans.), The Sacred Books of China: The Texts of Tâoism, Part I, Sacred Books of the East vol. XXXIX (Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1891), Book IV, par. 2, pp. 208-210" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:EmptyingOfTheHeart ;
    pm:situationGround "The three steps are a graded withdrawal from the sensory channel and are stated as such: not to wait for the hearing of the ears, then not even for the hearing of the mind. The practitioner is awake throughout; what is withdrawn is the reliance on what the ears report. That is the criterion this coordinate names."@en .

pmc:EthiopianAsmatGuardAgainstMen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Namen schützen vor Menschen"@de,
        "The names guard against men"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers written for Za-Walda Hawaryat (London: W. Griggs, 1900), section 4, p. LVII" ;
    skos:definition "The claim of the fourth section of the same roll: that the names shall enable a man to guard himself against evil men, liars, calumniators, men of wrath, the hand of him that would attack him, and the feet of those who would rush upon him by means of magical powers; and, in the petition that follows, that those who would oppose him in the time of judgment shall not do so."@en ;
    skos:note "Set beside the outcome of the sixth section because the two are of different kinds. Guarding oneself against liars has no moment at which it can be seen not to have happened; a spear turned back has one."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianBarya a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Barja"@de,
        "Barya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I, II, III, VI, VII, VIII, pp. 216-220" ;
    skos:altLabel "Borya"@en ;
    skos:definition "The being named more often than any other in the Meux roll, against whose evil eye the first, second, third, sixth, seventh and eighth prayers ask deliverance, and against whom the diseases enumerated in them are directed."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition read prints Barya and Borya for what is plainly one name and does not say which spelling stands in the parchment. Held as one naming with the second form as an alternate label rather than split into two, because nothing in the volume supports two bearers."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Barya" ;
    pm:nameRole "the bringer of the evil eye against which the prayers are said" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianOathOfWerzelya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Der Eid, auf dem der geschriebene Schutz ruht"@de,
        "The oath that grounds the written protection"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219" ;
    skos:definition "The undertaking sworn by the pierced child-killer, by the seven ranks of the archangels, that she will not contend against the Name, will never journey along the path where it is to be met with, or enter any church wherein it is commemorated, or any house or place whatsoever, and that this prayer shall be placed upon children, young men or the full grown, unto the end of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for a node of its own is that the roll states its own mechanism, which is uncommon. The protection is not a power the wearer acquires and not a favour asked each time; it is the enforcement of a promise extracted once from the adversary, and the written object is what puts the wearer inside the scope of that promise. A protocol that wanted to unsettle the claim would have to unsettle the oath, and there is no proceeding for that."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianOwnerNamedInEveryPrayer a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Rolle nennt die Person, für die sie geschrieben wurde"@de,
        "The roll names the person it was written for"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayers I-IX, pp. 216-220; the supplementary rolls, pp. LV-LXIV" ;
    skos:definition "The condition, visible in every prayer of the roll and never stated as a rule in it, that the protection is asked for a named person: each of the nine prayers of the Meux roll closes by naming the woman Aheta Mikael, and the three supplementary rolls each name their own owner in the same place."@en ;
    skos:note "Read off the wording and not from a statement about it. The volume nowhere says that a roll must carry its owner's name; it prints four rolls, and all four do it in every section. What follows for any protocol is that a failure is always available for explanation as a wrong or missing name, and that this escape is built into the object rather than argued for."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianSucklingProtectionPresupposesTheWearing a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Bewahrung setzt das Tragen über die Frist voraus"@de,
        "The preserving presupposes the wearing through the term"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity. The prayer says the writing down and the wearing must be done and gives the term, and it names no consequence of breaking off, so nothing here carries a citable consequence of skipping."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EthiopianWearingUntilChildbearingEnds ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianSusenyos a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Susenjos"@de,
        "Susenyos"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219" ;
    skos:altLabel "Socinius"@en ;
    skos:definition "The being named in the seventh prayer as the man whose child was killed, who mounted his horse Cherubim, took a spear in his right hand, was granted power from a voice out of heaven, and slew the killer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Susenyus" ;
    pm:nameRole "the saint on horseback whose prayer is written down and worn" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianWerzelya a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Werzelja"@de,
        "Werzelya"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 219" ;
    skos:altLabel "Ursula"@en ;
    skos:definition "The being named in the seventh prayer of the Meux roll as the one who came in the matter of the first child and killed him, and who after being pierced swears never again to approach where the Name is met with."@en ;
    skos:note "The edition read renders the name as Ursula in the introduction and prints Werzewelya in the translation. The identifier keeps the transliterated form of the roll and not the editor's substitution, which is a Latin name for a being the roll does not call by one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:nameForm "Werzewelya" ;
    pm:nameRole "the killer of the child at the breast, bound by her own oath" ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic .

pmc:EthiopianWornPrayerYieldsProtectionOfTheSuckling a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das getragene Gebet bewahrt den Säugling"@de,
        "The worn prayer preserves the suckling child"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Budge (ed.), The Magical Prayers of Aheta Mikael (London: W. Griggs, 1900), Lady Meux MS No. 5, prayer VII, p. 218" ;
    skos:note "The list of harms is long and the wording is a petition rather than a guarantee: it says preserve and protect, and it names the woman it is for. What makes the claim examinable at all is not the list but the term beside it, and what makes it hard is that infant death from any of the named causes can be assigned to a lapse in the wearing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EthiopianSusenyosPrayerWorn ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EthiopianOrthodoxMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EthiopianProtectionOfTheSuckling .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitAusschluss a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Mittel gilt nur für den, der noch nicht ins Feuer oder Wasser gefallen ist"@de,
        "The remedy holds only for one who has not yet fallen into fire or water"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" ;
    skos:note "An exclusion in the heading of a prescription is a different thing from an excuse after a failure. It stands before the application, it is determinable on the sufferer beforehand, and it divides the cases into two groups for which the book claims different things. That is what SPEC §15 asks of a failing condition and it is rare in this holding, which otherwise carries conditions that bite only afterwards."@en,
        "Whether the division is prognostic or self-protective is not decided here and cannot be decided from the wording. Falling into fire or water is the mark of a severe and manifest seizure disorder, so the clause may exclude exactly the cases in which the remedy would visibly fail. The book gives no reason, and the alternative is left standing rather than filled in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The heading states the exclusion as the scope of the prescription: it is a remedy for the falling sickness when one has not yet fallen into fire or water. Outside that case the book claims nothing, and the same heading stands a second time in the index of the Reading printing, so it is not a compositor's slip."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der angehängte Zettel nimmt die fallende Krankheit"@de,
        "The hung slip takes away the falling sickness"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 27",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 17" ;
    skos:note "The book carries a second remedy for the same affliction, without any such condition, some pages further on; the exclusion below therefore belongs to this prescription and not to the affliction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitZettel ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicFallendeKrankheitHeilung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesMondfenster a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen setzt das Fenster aus Wochentag und Mond voraus"@de,
        "The knotting presupposes the window of weekday and moon"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterPresupposesUnbeschrauen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen setzt voraus, dass es unbeschrauen geschieht"@de,
        "The knotting presupposes that it is done unbeschrauen"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    skos:note "Prescribed order and not stated necessity, because the passage gives the condition as part of the instruction and says nothing about what follows if it is broken. The book has consequences elsewhere and none here, and the difference is kept rather than filled in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterYieldsHeilung a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Knüpfen vertreibt die Gichter"@de,
        "The knotting drives out the fits"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 29",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 18" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterKnoten ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicGichterHeilung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallYieldsUebergang a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Die geteilte Luft trägt die Krankheit auf die Kuh hinüber"@de,
        "The shared air carries the disease over to the cow"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Ephrata, Pa., 1828), Anhang, S. 71-72",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), Appendix, pp. 59-60" ;
    skos:note "A handbook of charms citing a newspaper as its warrant is itself a finding about the transmission, and the warrant it gives is an appeal to European physicians and to repeated trials rather than to a charm. It is recorded here and not in dcterms:source, where SPEC §10 does not admit it."@en,
        "The passage is not Hohman's. The Reading printing of eighteen twenty has no appendix; the piece stands first in the Ephrata printing of eighteen twenty-eight, introduced as a remedy made public in the Lancaster newspapers in the year eighteen twenty-eight and closed with the name of the paper, the German-language Lancaster Adler. The Harrisburg translation of eighteen fifty renders that name as Lancaster Eagle, which is how the sheet is usually looked for and why it is usually not found."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhstallUebertragung ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKuhFaelltTodt .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBriefFrist a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "An welchem Freitag des Mondes der Brief angehängt wird"@de,
        "Which Friday of the moon the letter is hung on"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 53",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 32" ;
    skos:note "Kept as a dispute and not resolved. Resolving it would require the intervening American printings, which were not opened; that the translation is called literal on its own title page is a reason to record the divergence, not to prefer either side."@en,
        "The eighteen twenty printing sets the hanging on at the last Friday in the old light, that is at the end of the waning moon. The eighteen fifty translation sets it at the first Friday in the decreasing moon, which is the other end of the same quarter. The two cannot both be followed, and the difference falls on the only part of the prescription that could be missed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRheumaBrief ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelYieldsVorzeichen a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Das Auflegen bringt das Vorzeichen des Ausgangs hervor"@de,
        "The laying on brings out the sign of the outcome"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Die Schelwurzel«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 20" ;
    skos:note "The same paragraph attaches two further and quite different effects to the same herb: carried with the mole's heart it overcomes and ends all strife, and boiled and filtered its water is a wash for dim or shining eyes. Only the middle one is a prediction, and it is kept apart from the other two here rather than gathered with them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelProbe ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchelwurzelVorzeichen .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolZaehlungStrittig a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "An wem die sechzig Anwendungen geschahen"@de,
        "On whom the sixty applications were made"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 46",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 28" ;
    skos:note "The eighteen twenty printing says the author drove away his own severest toothaches more than sixty times. The eighteen fifty translation says he has cured the severest toothache more than sixty times and names no patient, which reads as a series over other people. The number is the same and the population it runs over is not, and a count whose population is unsettled cannot be compared with anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicVitriolYieldsStillung ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderKautel a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Den Busch wieder losmachen, sobald das Gut zurück ist"@de,
        "Loose the bush again once the goods are back"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), S. 90",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 57",
        "Romanus-Büchlein (Glatz: Franz Pompejus, 1788), Nr. 65, in the edition of Joseph H. Peterson, 2006" ;
    skos:definition "The warning to watch for the return of the stolen goods and, when they are back, to carry the stone to the place it lay and to loose the bush again."@en ;
    skos:note "A caution about what the operator owes after the effect has occurred, not about the risk of performing it. Both printings and the older booklet carry it in the same order and with the same clause; no consequence of leaving the bush bent is named in any of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicWachholderbusch ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisAlgaier a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "John Algaier in Reading, 1819"@de,
        "John Algaier of Reading, 1819"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 8" ;
    skos:note "A very sore finger with erysipelas. The author used for the erysipelas and for the healing of the finger; by the next morning the erysipelas was gone, the finger hardly hurt any more and began to heal strongly. The year is eighteen nineteen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRothlaufStillung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisArnold a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Arnolds Tochter bei Libanon, 1815"@de,
        "Arnold's daughter near Lebanon, 1815"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" ;
    skos:note "The handle broke off the pot while she was pouring, the hot coffee ran over her arm and burnt it rather badly. The author states that he was present and saw it; he took the burn out, the arm did not go sore and healed in a very short time. The master Arnold lives not far from Lebanon, Lebanon County; the year is eighteen fifteen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicBrandNehmen .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisAufnahmeregel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Die Zeugen sind vor einen Friedensrichter zu nötigen"@de,
        "The witnesses may be compelled before a justice of the peace"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" ;
    skos:definition "The rule of admission the section closes with: should any of the named witnesses, who received help through the author or through his wife and through God, call him a liar and say that they were not helped, when they themselves acknowledged to him that they were, then he will compel them to acknowledge it once more before a justice of the peace, if it is possible; and he believes for the greater part that this can be done."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause is also a limit on the claim it defends. The author offers to compel the acknowledgement, not to produce the cure; what he says can be tested before the justice is what the witness said, and not what happened to the eye."@en,
        "This is the part of the section that makes it more than a list. A transmission that names a forum in which its own witnesses can be made to repeat or withdraw their testimony has stated a procedure with an outcome that could have gone otherwise. Nothing else in the corpus does so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisBayer a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "John Bayer bei Reading, 1818"@de,
        "John Bayer near Reading, 1818"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 5" ;
    skos:note "The son of Jacob Bayer, living near Reading, had a running sore on the leg and suffered very great pain from it; the author attended him and in a short time the leg was healed, in the year eighteen eighteen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchmerzStillungAmGlied .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisBingemanBube a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Johann Bingemans Bube, Ruscombmanor, Spätjahr 1812"@de,
        "Johann Bingeman's boy, Ruscombmanor, autumn 1812"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 6" ;
    skos:note "Attested as a third-party ascription and not as a first-person report, because the author did not perform it. The acting person is his wife, and he reports what she did. The section holds two such cases and thirteen of his own, and the difference is kept rather than levelled."@en,
        "The boy had burnt himself severely and mortification had already set in. The author's wife came there by chance in the late autumn of eighteen twelve, used for it, and the mortification was driven out in a short time; the boy was soon restored and healed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKalterBrandVertreibung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisBingemanFrau a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Johann Bingemans Frau, Ruscombmanor, Spätjahr 1812"@de,
        "Johann Bingeman's wife, Ruscombmanor, autumn 1812"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 6" ;
    skos:note "At about the same time the author's wife stilled a strong erysipelas on a sore leg of Bingeman's wife. Reported in a single sentence beside the boy's case and kept as its own node because it is a second patient, a second affliction and a second outcome."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRothlaufStillung .

