pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisHartman a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Michael Hartmans jun. Kind, Elsaß Taunschip"@de,
        "Michael Hartman junior's child, Alsace Township"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 6" ;
    skos:note "A child with a very sore mouth; the author attended it and in a little more than twenty-four hours it was helped. No year is given."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchmerzStillungAmGlied .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisJorger a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Henrich Jörgers Bube, 1814"@de,
        "Henrich Jorger's boy, 1814"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 5" ;
    skos:note "Henrich Jorger, at the time of writing resident in Reading, brought a boy in eighteen fourteen who was in astonishing pain, again from a shot-blister on the eye; in a little more than twenty-four hours he was helped."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicSchussblatterHeilung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisJunckenBacken a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Der Rothlauf am Backen von Junckens Sohnesfrau, 1819"@de,
        "The erysipelas on John Juncken's son's wife's cheek, 1819"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), p. 7" ;
    skos:note "Beside the headache she had a sore cheek with an astonishing erysipelas on it, for which she had already lain some days in bed. The erysipelas went in some seven or nine hours; the cheek broke open and healed strongly."@en,
        "The shortest interval named anywhere in the section, and the only one given as a range. Seven or nine hours is a measurement of the sort that can be missed, and it is the reason this case is separated from the headache it is reported with."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicRothlaufStillung .

pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicZeugnisJunckenTochter a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Johann Junckens Tochter und Sohnesfrau, Nackenmixen, 1819"@de,
        "John Juncken's daughter and son's wife, Nackenmixen, 1819"@en ;
    dcterms:source "Johann Georg Hohman, Der lange verborgene Freund (Reading, Pa., 1820), Abschnitt »Zeugniße«",
        "John George Hohman, The Long Lost Friend (Harrisburg, Pa., 1850), pp. 6-7" ;
    skos:note "Both had violent pain in the head, and through the author it went. The family lives at Nackenmixen, near the Heckack, Bucks County; the year is eighteen nineteen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:EuropeanFolkMagicGebrauchen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:EuropeanFolkMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicKopfwehStillung .

